window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2024-06-1", "source": "2024年06月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose your university is seeking students’ opinions on whether university libraries should be open to the public. You are now to write an essay to express your view. You will have 30 minutes for the task. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "Whether university libraries should open their doors to the public has sparked heated debate in recent years. In\nmy view, these libraries should indeed be accessible to ordinary citizens, provided that reasonable management\nmeasures are in place.\nTwo reasons support my position. Firstly, opening university libraries promotes the sharing of knowledge.\nMany valuable books and academic resources sit idle on the shelves, and allowing the public to use them turns\ncampus libraries into vibrant centers of lifelong learning. Moreover, such openness enriches the cultural life of\nthe whole community. For instance, a retired worker or a curious teenager could freely explore fields they\nnever studied at school, which narrows the gap between universities and society.\nAdmittedly, outside visitors may strain limited seats and disturb students. Yet this problem can be eased by\nsetting fixed visiting hours and registration rules. Therefore, I firmly believe university libraries should\nwelcome the public, so long as students' study needs are fully protected."}, "translation": {"prompt": "四合院(siheyuan)是中国一种传统的住宅建筑，其特点是房屋建造在一个院子的四周，将院子合围在中间。四合院通常冬暖夏凉，环境舒适，尤其适合大家庭居住。四合院在中国各地有多种类型，其中以北京的四合院最为典型。如今，随着现代城市的发展，传统的四合院已逐渐减少，但因其独特的建筑风格，四合院对中国文化的传承和中国历史的研究具有重要意义。", "sample": "The siheyuan is a traditional Chinese residential building, characterized by houses built on the four sides of a courtyard, enclosing it in the middle. It is usually warm in winter and cool in summer, providing a comfortable environment that is especially suitable for large families. There are various types of siheyuan across China, among which the ones in Beijing are the most typical. Nowadays, with the development of modern cities, traditional siheyuan have gradually decreased; however, owing to their unique architectural style, they remain of great significance to the inheritance of Chinese culture and the study of Chinese history."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2024-06-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "It's well known that physical exercise is beneficial not just to physical health but also to mental health. Yet whereas most countries have  __26__  , evidence-backed guidelines on the type and intensity of exercise  __27__  for various physical health benefits, such guidelines do not yet exist for exercise and mood. This is  __28__  due to a lack of necessary evidence. However, a new systematic review brings us usefully up to-date on the current findings in this area.\nBefore  __29__  into some of the key take-aways, an important  __30__  made in the review is between aerobic exercise and anaerobic. The former  __31__  such things as walking, jogging and cycling and means exercising in such a way that your body is able to use oxygen to burn fat for energy. In contrast, anaerobic exercise—such as lifting heavy weights—is of such  __32__  intensity that your body does not have time to use oxygen to create energy and so instead it breaks down glucose(葡萄糖) in your blood or muscles.\nBeginning first with the influence of exercise intensity on the mood benefits of aerobic exercise, the researchers, led by John Chan at Shenzhen University, found  __33__  results from 19 relevant studies. Some favoured higher intensity, others low, while seven studies found that intensity made no  __34__  to mood benefits.\nIn relation to the intensity of anaerobic exercise, however, the results were far clearer—the optimum(最佳选择) for improving mood is  __35__  intensity, perhaps because low intensity is too dull while high intensity is too unpleasant.", "matchIntro": "Why Do Americans Work So Much?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "How will we all keep busy when we only have to work 15 hours a week? That was the question that worried the British economist John Maynard Keynes when he wrote his short essay \"Economic Possibilities for Our Grandchildren\" in 1930. Over the next century, he predicted, the economy would become so productive that people would barely need to work at all. For a while, it looked like Keynes was right. In 1930 the average working week was 47 hours in the United States. But by 1970, the number of hours Americans worked on average had fallen to slightly less than 39."}, {"label": "B", "text": "But then something changed. Instead of continuing to decline, the duration of the working week remained stable. It has stayed at just below 40 hours for nearly five decades. So what happened? Why are people working just as much today as in 1970?"}, {"label": "C", "text": "There would be no mystery in this if Keynes had been wrong about the power of technology to increase the economy's productivity, which he thought would lead to a standard of living \"between four and eight times as high as it is today.\" But Keynes got that right: Technology has made the economy massively more productive. According to Benjamin M.Friedman, an economist at Harvard, the U.S. economy is right on track to reach Keynes's eight-fold(八倍) multiple by 2029. That is a century after the last data Keynes would have had access to."}, {"label": "D", "text": "In a new paper, Friedman tries to figure out why that increased productivity has not translated into increased leisure time. Perhaps people just never feel materially satisfied, always wanting more money to buy the next new thing. This is a theory that appeals to many economists. \"This argument is, at best, far from sufficient,\" he writes. If that were the case, why did the duration of the working week decline in the first place?"}, {"label": "E", "text": "Another theory Friedman considers is that, in an era of ever fewer settings that provide effective opportunities for personal connections and relationships, people may place more value on the socializing that happens at work. There is support for this theory. Many people today consider colleagues as friends. But Friedman argues that the evidence for this theory is far from conclusive. Many workers report that they would like to spend more time with family, rather than at work. Furthermore, this theory cannot explain the change in trend in the U.S. working week in the 1970s."}, {"label": "F", "text": "A third possibility proves more convincing for Friedman. That is: American inequality means that the gains of increasing productivity are not widely shared by everyone. In other words, most Americans are too poor to work less. Unlike the other two explanations Friedman considers, this one fits chronologically(按年代). Inequality declined in America during the period following World War II, along with the duration of the working week. But since the early 1970s it has risen dramatically."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Keynes's prediction of a shorter working week rests on the idea that the standard of living would continue rising for everyone. But Friedman says that this is not what has happened. Although Keynes's eight-fold figure holds up for the economy as a whole, it is not at all the case for the median(中位数的) American worker. For them, output by 2029 is likely to be around 3.5 times what it was when Keynes was writing. This is a bit below his four-to eight-fold predicted range."}, {"label": "H", "text": "This can be seen in the median worker's income over this time period, complete with a shift in 1973 that fits in precisely with when the working week stopped shrinking. According to Friedman, between 1947 and 1973 the average hourly wage for normal workers (those who were not in management roles) in private industries other than agriculture nearly doubled in terms of what their money could buy. But by 2013 the average hourly wage for ordinary workers had fallen 5 percent from the 1973 level in terms of actual purchasing power. Thus, though American incomes may have gone up since 1973, the amount that American workers can actually buy with their money has gone down. For most Americans, then, the magic of increasing productivity stopped working around 1973. Thus, they had to keep working just as much in order to maintain their standard of living."}, {"label": "I", "text": "What Keynes predicted was a very optimistic version of what economists call technological unemployment. This is the idea that less labor will be necessary because machines can do so much. In Keynes's vision, the resulting unemployment would be distributed more or less evenly across society in the form of increased leisure. But Friedman says that, for Americans, reality is much darker. Americans now have a labor market in which millions of people—those with fewer skills and less education—are seeking whatever poorly paid work they can get. This is confirmed by a recent poll that found that, for half of hourly workers, their top concern is not that they work too much but that they work too little. This is most likely not because they like their jobs so much. Rather, we can assume it is because they need the money."}, {"label": "J", "text": "This explanation leaves an important question. If the very rich—the workers who have reaped above-average gains from the increased productivity since Keynes's time—can afford to work less, why do they continue to work so much? (Indeed, research has shown that the highest earners in America tend to work the most.) Friedman believes that for many top earners, work is a labor of love. They are doing work they care about and are interested in, and doing more of it is not necessarily a burden. For them, it may even be a pleasure. These top earners derive meaning from their jobs and work is an important part of how they think of themselves. And, of course, they are compensated for it at a level that makes it worth their while."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Friedman concludes that the prosperity(繁荣) Keynes predicted is here. After all, the economy as a whole has grown even more brilliantly than he expected. But for most Americans, that prosperity is nowhere to be seen. And, as a result, neither are those shorter working weeks."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Lao Zi once said, \"Care about what other people think and you will always be their prisoner.\"\nPeople-pleasing, or seeking self-worth through others' approval, is unproductive and an exhausting way to go through life. Why do we allow what others think of us to have so much power over how we feel about ourselves? If it's true that you can't please all people all of the time, wouldn't it make sense to stop trying?\nUnfortunately, sense often isn't driving our behavior. For social beings who desire love and belonging, wanting to be liked, and caring about the effect we have on others, is healthy and allows us to make connections. However, where we get into trouble is when our self-worth is dependent upon whether we win someone's approval or not.\nThis need to be liked can be traced back to when we were children and were completely dependent on others to take care of us: Small children are not just learning how to walk and communicate, they are also trying to learn how the world works. We learn about who we are and what is expected of us based on interactions with others, so, to a four-year-old, if Mommy or Daddy doesn't like him or her, there is the danger that they will abandon them. We need to understand that when we desperately want someone to approve of us, it's being driven by that little kid part of us that is still terrified of abandonment.\nAs you become more capable of providing yourself with the approval you seek, your need for external validation will start to vanish, leaving you stronger, more confident, and yes, happier in your life. Imagine how much time we lose each moment we restrain our authentic selves in an effort to be liked.\nIf we base our worth on the opinions of others, we cheat ourselves of the power to shape our experiences and embrace life not only for others but also for ourselves, because ultimately, there is no difference. So embrace the cliché(老话) and love yourself as it's highly doubtful that you'll regret it.", "47": "Lao Zi once said, \"Care about what other people think and you will always be their prisoner.\"\nPeople-pleasing, or seeking self-worth through others' approval, is unproductive and an exhausting way to go through life. Why do we allow what others think of us to have so much power over how we feel about ourselves? If it's true that you can't please all people all of the time, wouldn't it make sense to stop trying?\nUnfortunately, sense often isn't driving our behavior. For social beings who desire love and belonging, wanting to be liked, and caring about the effect we have on others, is healthy and allows us to make connections. However, where we get into trouble is when our self-worth is dependent upon whether we win someone's approval or not.\nThis need to be liked can be traced back to when we were children and were completely dependent on others to take care of us: Small children are not just learning how to walk and communicate, they are also trying to learn how the world works. We learn about who we are and what is expected of us based on interactions with others, so, to a four-year-old, if Mommy or Daddy doesn't like him or her, there is the danger that they will abandon them. We need to understand that when we desperately want someone to approve of us, it's being driven by that little kid part of us that is still terrified of abandonment.\nAs you become more capable of providing yourself with the approval you seek, your need for external validation will start to vanish, leaving you stronger, more confident, and yes, happier in your life. Imagine how much time we lose each moment we restrain our authentic selves in an effort to be liked.\nIf we base our worth on the opinions of others, we cheat ourselves of the power to shape our experiences and embrace life not only for others but also for ourselves, because ultimately, there is no difference. So embrace the cliché(老话) and love yourself as it's highly doubtful that you'll regret it.", "48": "Lao Zi once said, \"Care about what other people think and you will always be their prisoner.\"\nPeople-pleasing, or seeking self-worth through others' approval, is unproductive and an exhausting way to go through life. Why do we allow what others think of us to have so much power over how we feel about ourselves? If it's true that you can't please all people all of the time, wouldn't it make sense to stop trying?\nUnfortunately, sense often isn't driving our behavior. For social beings who desire love and belonging, wanting to be liked, and caring about the effect we have on others, is healthy and allows us to make connections. However, where we get into trouble is when our self-worth is dependent upon whether we win someone's approval or not.\nThis need to be liked can be traced back to when we were children and were completely dependent on others to take care of us: Small children are not just learning how to walk and communicate, they are also trying to learn how the world works. We learn about who we are and what is expected of us based on interactions with others, so, to a four-year-old, if Mommy or Daddy doesn't like him or her, there is the danger that they will abandon them. We need to understand that when we desperately want someone to approve of us, it's being driven by that little kid part of us that is still terrified of abandonment.\nAs you become more capable of providing yourself with the approval you seek, your need for external validation will start to vanish, leaving you stronger, more confident, and yes, happier in your life. Imagine how much time we lose each moment we restrain our authentic selves in an effort to be liked.\nIf we base our worth on the opinions of others, we cheat ourselves of the power to shape our experiences and embrace life not only for others but also for ourselves, because ultimately, there is no difference. So embrace the cliché(老话) and love yourself as it's highly doubtful that you'll regret it.", "49": "Lao Zi once said, \"Care about what other people think and you will always be their prisoner.\"\nPeople-pleasing, or seeking self-worth through others' approval, is unproductive and an exhausting way to go through life. Why do we allow what others think of us to have so much power over how we feel about ourselves? If it's true that you can't please all people all of the time, wouldn't it make sense to stop trying?\nUnfortunately, sense often isn't driving our behavior. For social beings who desire love and belonging, wanting to be liked, and caring about the effect we have on others, is healthy and allows us to make connections. However, where we get into trouble is when our self-worth is dependent upon whether we win someone's approval or not.\nThis need to be liked can be traced back to when we were children and were completely dependent on others to take care of us: Small children are not just learning how to walk and communicate, they are also trying to learn how the world works. We learn about who we are and what is expected of us based on interactions with others, so, to a four-year-old, if Mommy or Daddy doesn't like him or her, there is the danger that they will abandon them. We need to understand that when we desperately want someone to approve of us, it's being driven by that little kid part of us that is still terrified of abandonment.\nAs you become more capable of providing yourself with the approval you seek, your need for external validation will start to vanish, leaving you stronger, more confident, and yes, happier in your life. Imagine how much time we lose each moment we restrain our authentic selves in an effort to be liked.\nIf we base our worth on the opinions of others, we cheat ourselves of the power to shape our experiences and embrace life not only for others but also for ourselves, because ultimately, there is no difference. So embrace the cliché(老话) and love yourself as it's highly doubtful that you'll regret it.", "50": "Lao Zi once said, \"Care about what other people think and you will always be their prisoner.\"\nPeople-pleasing, or seeking self-worth through others' approval, is unproductive and an exhausting way to go through life. Why do we allow what others think of us to have so much power over how we feel about ourselves? If it's true that you can't please all people all of the time, wouldn't it make sense to stop trying?\nUnfortunately, sense often isn't driving our behavior. For social beings who desire love and belonging, wanting to be liked, and caring about the effect we have on others, is healthy and allows us to make connections. However, where we get into trouble is when our self-worth is dependent upon whether we win someone's approval or not.\nThis need to be liked can be traced back to when we were children and were completely dependent on others to take care of us: Small children are not just learning how to walk and communicate, they are also trying to learn how the world works. We learn about who we are and what is expected of us based on interactions with others, so, to a four-year-old, if Mommy or Daddy doesn't like him or her, there is the danger that they will abandon them. We need to understand that when we desperately want someone to approve of us, it's being driven by that little kid part of us that is still terrified of abandonment.\nAs you become more capable of providing yourself with the approval you seek, your need for external validation will start to vanish, leaving you stronger, more confident, and yes, happier in your life. Imagine how much time we lose each moment we restrain our authentic selves in an effort to be liked.\nIf we base our worth on the opinions of others, we cheat ourselves of the power to shape our experiences and embrace life not only for others but also for ourselves, because ultimately, there is no difference. So embrace the cliché(老话) and love yourself as it's highly doubtful that you'll regret it.", "51": "Some people have said aging is more a slide into forgetfulness than a journey towards wisdom. However, a growing body of research suggests that late-in-life learning is possible. In reality, education does an aging brain good.\nThroughout life, people's brains constantly renovate themselves. In the late 1960s, British brain scientist Geoffrey Raisman spied growth in damaged brain regions of rats through an electron microscope; their brains were forging new connections. This meant brains may change every time a person learns something new.\nOf course, that doesn't mean the brain isn't affected by the effects of time. Just as height usually declines over the years, so does brain volume: Humans lose about 4 percent every decade starting in their 40s. But that reduction doesn't necessarily make people think slower; as long as we are alive and functioning, we can alter our brains with new information and experiences.\nIn fact, scientists now suspect accumulating novel experiences, facts, and skills can keep people's minds more flexible. New pathways can strengthen our ever-changing mental structure, even as the brain shrinks.\nConventional fixes like word puzzles and brain-training apps can contribute to mental durability. Even something as simple as taking a different route to the grocery store or going somewhere new on vacation can keep the brain healthy.\nA desire for new life challenges can further boost brainpower. Research about aging adults who take on new enterprises shows improved function and memory as well as a reduced risk of mental disease. Openness—a characteristic defined by curiosity and a desire for knowledge—may also help folks pass brain tests. Some folks are born with this take-in-the-world attitude, but those who aren't as genetically gifted aren't necessarily out of luck. While genes can encourage an interest in doing new things, a 2012 study in the journal Psychology and Aging found completing reasoning tasks like puzzles and number games can enhance that desire for novel experiences, which can, in turn, refresh the brain. That's why brain scientist Richard Kennedy says \"It's not that old dogs can't learn new tricks. It's that maybe old dogs don't realize why they should.\"", "52": "Some people have said aging is more a slide into forgetfulness than a journey towards wisdom. However, a growing body of research suggests that late-in-life learning is possible. In reality, education does an aging brain good.\nThroughout life, people's brains constantly renovate themselves. In the late 1960s, British brain scientist Geoffrey Raisman spied growth in damaged brain regions of rats through an electron microscope; their brains were forging new connections. This meant brains may change every time a person learns something new.\nOf course, that doesn't mean the brain isn't affected by the effects of time. Just as height usually declines over the years, so does brain volume: Humans lose about 4 percent every decade starting in their 40s. But that reduction doesn't necessarily make people think slower; as long as we are alive and functioning, we can alter our brains with new information and experiences.\nIn fact, scientists now suspect accumulating novel experiences, facts, and skills can keep people's minds more flexible. New pathways can strengthen our ever-changing mental structure, even as the brain shrinks.\nConventional fixes like word puzzles and brain-training apps can contribute to mental durability. Even something as simple as taking a different route to the grocery store or going somewhere new on vacation can keep the brain healthy.\nA desire for new life challenges can further boost brainpower. Research about aging adults who take on new enterprises shows improved function and memory as well as a reduced risk of mental disease. Openness—a characteristic defined by curiosity and a desire for knowledge—may also help folks pass brain tests. Some folks are born with this take-in-the-world attitude, but those who aren't as genetically gifted aren't necessarily out of luck. While genes can encourage an interest in doing new things, a 2012 study in the journal Psychology and Aging found completing reasoning tasks like puzzles and number games can enhance that desire for novel experiences, which can, in turn, refresh the brain. That's why brain scientist Richard Kennedy says \"It's not that old dogs can't learn new tricks. It's that maybe old dogs don't realize why they should.\"", "53": "Some people have said aging is more a slide into forgetfulness than a journey towards wisdom. However, a growing body of research suggests that late-in-life learning is possible. In reality, education does an aging brain good.\nThroughout life, people's brains constantly renovate themselves. In the late 1960s, British brain scientist Geoffrey Raisman spied growth in damaged brain regions of rats through an electron microscope; their brains were forging new connections. This meant brains may change every time a person learns something new.\nOf course, that doesn't mean the brain isn't affected by the effects of time. Just as height usually declines over the years, so does brain volume: Humans lose about 4 percent every decade starting in their 40s. But that reduction doesn't necessarily make people think slower; as long as we are alive and functioning, we can alter our brains with new information and experiences.\nIn fact, scientists now suspect accumulating novel experiences, facts, and skills can keep people's minds more flexible. New pathways can strengthen our ever-changing mental structure, even as the brain shrinks.\nConventional fixes like word puzzles and brain-training apps can contribute to mental durability. Even something as simple as taking a different route to the grocery store or going somewhere new on vacation can keep the brain healthy.\nA desire for new life challenges can further boost brainpower. Research about aging adults who take on new enterprises shows improved function and memory as well as a reduced risk of mental disease. Openness—a characteristic defined by curiosity and a desire for knowledge—may also help folks pass brain tests. Some folks are born with this take-in-the-world attitude, but those who aren't as genetically gifted aren't necessarily out of luck. While genes can encourage an interest in doing new things, a 2012 study in the journal Psychology and Aging found completing reasoning tasks like puzzles and number games can enhance that desire for novel experiences, which can, in turn, refresh the brain. That's why brain scientist Richard Kennedy says \"It's not that old dogs can't learn new tricks. It's that maybe old dogs don't realize why they should.\"", "54": "Some people have said aging is more a slide into forgetfulness than a journey towards wisdom. However, a growing body of research suggests that late-in-life learning is possible. In reality, education does an aging brain good.\nThroughout life, people's brains constantly renovate themselves. In the late 1960s, British brain scientist Geoffrey Raisman spied growth in damaged brain regions of rats through an electron microscope; their brains were forging new connections. This meant brains may change every time a person learns something new.\nOf course, that doesn't mean the brain isn't affected by the effects of time. Just as height usually declines over the years, so does brain volume: Humans lose about 4 percent every decade starting in their 40s. But that reduction doesn't necessarily make people think slower; as long as we are alive and functioning, we can alter our brains with new information and experiences.\nIn fact, scientists now suspect accumulating novel experiences, facts, and skills can keep people's minds more flexible. New pathways can strengthen our ever-changing mental structure, even as the brain shrinks.\nConventional fixes like word puzzles and brain-training apps can contribute to mental durability. Even something as simple as taking a different route to the grocery store or going somewhere new on vacation can keep the brain healthy.\nA desire for new life challenges can further boost brainpower. Research about aging adults who take on new enterprises shows improved function and memory as well as a reduced risk of mental disease. Openness—a characteristic defined by curiosity and a desire for knowledge—may also help folks pass brain tests. Some folks are born with this take-in-the-world attitude, but those who aren't as genetically gifted aren't necessarily out of luck. While genes can encourage an interest in doing new things, a 2012 study in the journal Psychology and Aging found completing reasoning tasks like puzzles and number games can enhance that desire for novel experiences, which can, in turn, refresh the brain. That's why brain scientist Richard Kennedy says \"It's not that old dogs can't learn new tricks. It's that maybe old dogs don't realize why they should.\"", "55": "Some people have said aging is more a slide into forgetfulness than a journey towards wisdom. However, a growing body of research suggests that late-in-life learning is possible. In reality, education does an aging brain good.\nThroughout life, people's brains constantly renovate themselves. In the late 1960s, British brain scientist Geoffrey Raisman spied growth in damaged brain regions of rats through an electron microscope; their brains were forging new connections. This meant brains may change every time a person learns something new.\nOf course, that doesn't mean the brain isn't affected by the effects of time. Just as height usually declines over the years, so does brain volume: Humans lose about 4 percent every decade starting in their 40s. But that reduction doesn't necessarily make people think slower; as long as we are alive and functioning, we can alter our brains with new information and experiences.\nIn fact, scientists now suspect accumulating novel experiences, facts, and skills can keep people's minds more flexible. New pathways can strengthen our ever-changing mental structure, even as the brain shrinks.\nConventional fixes like word puzzles and brain-training apps can contribute to mental durability. Even something as simple as taking a different route to the grocery store or going somewhere new on vacation can keep the brain healthy.\nA desire for new life challenges can further boost brainpower. Research about aging adults who take on new enterprises shows improved function and memory as well as a reduced risk of mental disease. Openness—a characteristic defined by curiosity and a desire for knowledge—may also help folks pass brain tests. Some folks are born with this take-in-the-world attitude, but those who aren't as genetically gifted aren't necessarily out of luck. While genes can encourage an interest in doing new things, a 2012 study in the journal Psychology and Aging found completing reasoning tasks like puzzles and number games can enhance that desire for novel experiences, which can, in turn, refresh the brain. That's why brain scientist Richard Kennedy says \"It's not that old dogs can't learn new tricks. It's that maybe old dogs don't realize why they should.\""}, "questions": [{"id": "2024-06-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "Why did the six residents have to find another place to stay?", "options": ["A) Due to a fire alarm in their apartments.", "B) Because of the smoke and heat damage.", "C) Due to the water used to extinguish the flames.", "D) Because of the collapse of the three-story building."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Because of the smoke and heat damage.\n【定位】Due to smoke and heat damage, the four apartments were declared uninhabitable, and the six\nresidents had to move to another place, officials said.\n由于烟雾和高温造成的损坏，这四套公寓被判定为不适宜居住，六位居民因此不得不搬到别\n处，官方表示。\n【信号】因果信号 Due to 引出原因。答案句以 Due to smoke and heat damage 开头，Due to\n是明确的因果信号，紧接着就是结果 the six residents had to move to another place\n——「原因 + 结果」同句出现，这一句就是答案句。\n【替换】Due to smoke and heat damage ↔ Because of the smoke and heat damage\nDue to 与 Because of 同义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说因 a fire alarm in their apartments（公寓里的火警铃）。录音确实通篇讲 fire（火\n灾），但从没说居民因为「火警铃响」才搬走，搬离的真正原因是烟热损坏。它借你对 fire\n的耳熟来骗选。\n借用原文：a fire broke out in a building\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说因 the water used to extinguish the flames（灭火用的水）。录音里确有\nextinguishing the flames（扑灭火焰）这个动作，但只讲了灭火过程，从没说「水渍」让\n公寓不能住。它盗用 extinguish the flames 制造熟悉感。\n借用原文：extinguishing the flames\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说因 the collapse of the three-story building（三层楼坍塌）。录音确有 the three-\nstory building（三层楼）这个词，但全文只字未提坍塌，反而说 No injuries were\nreported（无人受伤）。它盗用 three-story building，再编一个没发生的「坍塌」。\n借用原文：the three-story building", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the news report say the Norwalk Fire Marshal is doing?", "options": ["A) Investigating the cause of the incident.", "B) Helping search for the suspect of the crime.", "C) Rescuing the businessmen trapped in the building.", "D) Checking town records for the property developer."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Investigating the cause of the incident.\n【定位】The Norwalk Fire Marshal is investigating the cause and origin of the fire.\n诺沃克消防局长正在调查这场火灾的起因和源头。\n【信号】主体名 Fire Marshal 后的动作。录音末句直接出现题目要找的主体 The Norwalk Fire\nMarshal，紧跟其后的动词 is investigating the cause and origin of the fire 就是它正在\n做的事——主体名 + 进行时动作，这句即答案句。\n【替换】investigating the cause and origin of the fire ↔ Investigating the cause of the incident\nthe fire 概括为 the incident\n【排除】B) 未提及\nB 说在 Helping search for the suspect of the crime（帮忙搜捕嫌犯）。录音通篇是火灾\n事故，没有任何「嫌犯 / 犯罪」字眼，消防局长做的是调查起因，不是抓人。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说在 Rescuing the businessmen trapped in the building（营救被困商人）。录音确实\n提到一楼有 commercial businesses（商铺），但明说 No injuries were reported（无人\n受伤），根本没人被困需救。它借 businesses 谐想出 businessmen 来诱选。\n借用原文：commercial businesses\nD) 张冠李戴\nD 说在 Checking town records for the property developer（为开发商查市镇记录）。\n录音确有 Town records list four apartments（市镇记录列有四套公寓）这一句，但 town\nrecords 只是新闻引用的信息来源，消防局长做的是调查起因，没人「为开发商查记录」。\n借用原文：Town records list four apartments in the building\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 A new study has cast doubt on historic research suggesting that the season or month of someone's\nbirth is associated with an increased risk of certain mental health conditions. The study looks at\nsymptoms of anxiety and depression among more than 70,000 older adults in Europe.\n一项新研究对历史研究提出了质疑,这些历史研究认为某人的出生季节或月份与某些心理健康状况的风险增\n加有关。该研究调查了欧洲超过7万名老年人的焦虑和抑郁症状。\n2 A number of past studies have found the link between season of birth and mental health diagnoses.\nResearchers have suggested that such links could arise from various things. These include nutrient intake,\nsun exposure, climate, and disease exposure varying across the course of the year. However, evidence\nhas been mixed. More recent studies have suggested that factors such as social class or economic\nbackground have more to do with these diagnoses than month of birth.\n过去的许多研究发现了出生季节与心理健康诊断之间的联系。研究人员认为,这种联系可能源于各种因素。\n这些因素包括全年不同时期的营养摄入、阳光照射、气候和疾病暴露的变化。然而,证据一直是混杂的。最\n近的研究表明,社会阶层或经济背景等因素与这些诊断的关系比出生月份更大。\n3 Overall, the new study found no significant relationship between participants' month of birth and\nsymptoms of depression or anxiety. There was some variability in some countries. In Poland, depressive\nsymptoms fluctuated a little depending on birth month. In the Czech Republic, the same was true of\nanxiety symptoms. But on the whole, there was no systematic pattern.\n总体而言,这项新研究发现参与者的出生月份与抑郁或焦虑症状之间没有显著关系。在一些国家存在一些变\n化。在波兰,抑郁症状根据出生月份有轻微波动。在捷克共和国,焦虑症状也是如此。但总的来说,没有系统\n性模式。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What have a number of past studies found about season of birth?", "options": ["A) It plays a less important role in one's health than nutrient intake.", "B) It impacts people's health to a lesser degree than sun exposure.", "C) It is associated with people's mental health conditions.", "D) It is linked with older adults' symptoms of depression."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It is associated with people's mental health conditions.\n【定位】A number of past studies have found the link between season of birth and mental\nhealth diagnoses.\n若干过往研究已发现出生季节与心理健康诊断之间存在关联。\n【信号】主语原样复现作信号。题干主语 a number of past studies 在录音第 2 段以 A number of\npast studies have found 几乎原样出现，这一「问谁就听谁」的主体复现就是定位信号：\n被问主体一出现，紧跟其后的 found the link… 即为答案句。\n【替换】the link between season of birth and mental health diagnoses ↔\nassociated with people's mental health conditions\nlink=associated、diagnoses≈conditions\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 用了录音第 2 段确实念到的 nutrient intake 一词，但录音只是把它列为「关联可能的成\n因之一」，从没说出生季节「在健康中作用不及营养摄入」。A 把它编成一句子虚乌有的轻\n重比较，是听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：These include nutrient intake, sun exposure, climate, and disease exposure\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 借用录音里同样念到的 sun exposure，谎称出生季节「对健康的影响不及日晒」。录音\n把 sun exposure 与 nutrient intake 并列为可能成因，并未拿它和出生季节比影响大小，\n属偷换概念式的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：These include nutrient intake, sun exposure, climate, and disease exposure\nD) 张冠李戴\nD 说关联的是 older adults' symptoms of depression。录音中「老年人」与「抑郁/焦虑\n症状」出现在介绍新研究样本（more than 70,000 older adults）与总结处，并非「过往\n研究就出生季节发现的关联对象」；过往研究发现的是更宽的 mental health diagnoses。\nD 把答案的范围从「心理健康」窄化到「老年人的抑郁症状」，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：symptoms of anxiety and depression among more than 70,000 older adults", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What did the new study find about the relationship between participants' month of birth and symptoms of depression?", "options": ["A) It was indefinite.", "B) It was systematic.", "C) It was straightforward.", "D) It was insignificant."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It was insignificant.\n【定位】Overall, the new study found no significant relationship between participants' month of birth\nand symptoms of depression or anxiety.\n总体而言，这项新研究发现参与者的出生月份与抑郁或焦虑症状之间没有显著关联。\n【信号】总结词 Overall + 否定 no significant。段首的 Overall 是结论信号，提示这句是新研究的\n总判断；其后的 no significant relationship 用「否定 + 程度词」给出定性——这种\n「Overall/否定句」往往就是答案落点，前面铺垫的别国小波动都是干扰。\n【替换】no significant relationship ↔ It was insignificant.\nno significant=insignificant\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nA 说 It was indefinite.（不确定的）。录音其实给出了明确结论——「没有显著关联」「没\n有系统性规律」，态度是确定的「无」，并非含糊不定。把「确定地没有」错听成「不确\n定」，是对结论性质的误判。\nB) 与原文相反\nB 用了录音末句确实念到的 systematic，可原文是 no systematic pattern（没有系统性规\n律），带否定。B 抹掉 no，单取 systematic 说成「它是系统性的」，正好与原文意思相\n反，是典型听到原词就选、却漏听否定词的陷阱。\n借用原文：there was no systematic pattern\nC) 未提及\nC 说 It was straightforward.（直接明了的）。录音从未用「直接/明了」评价这关系，只\n说「无显著关联、无系统规律，部分国家有些小波动」。这个定性原文根本没出现，属凭空\n臆造。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do the researchers' tests show about the cloned dog?", "options": ["A) It has helped solve several murder cases.", "B) It has become a star office dog in Beijing.", "C) It has surpassed its mother in performance.", "D) It has done better than naturally born dogs."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It has done better than naturally born dogs.\n【定位】The clone has already performed better than traditionally bred dogs on several tests.\n这只克隆犬在几项测试中已经表现得比传统繁育的狗更好。\n【信号】tests一词复现即落点。题干问的是 tests 显示什么，录音第一段末句 performed better\nthan traditionally bred dogs on several tests 正好出现 tests，又给出明确比较结果，这\n种'题干关键词在原文复现+给出结论'的句子就是答案句。\n【替换】traditionally bred dogs ↔ naturally born dogs\n传统繁育=自然出生\nperformed better ↔ done better\nperformed=done同义\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nIt has helped solve several murder cases. 偷用了录音里 helped solve multiple\nmurders 的'破案'信息，但原文说破案的是 The mother dog（母亲），不是这只克隆犬，\n把母亲的功劳安到了克隆犬头上。\n借用原文：The mother dog helped solve multiple murders and many other crimes.\nB) 原词陷阱\nIt has become a star office dog in Beijing. 拼接了录音里的 star（明星）和 Beijing（北\n京）两个原词，但原文只说克隆犬生于北京实验室、母亲是明星警犬，从没说克隆犬变成\n了'办公室明星狗'，属拼凑+无中生有。\n借用原文：The clone was born in a laboratory in Beijing in December.\nC) 偷换概念\nIt has surpassed its mother in performance. 利用了原文反复提到的 mother（母亲），\n但录音说克隆犬与母亲 almost identical genetically（基因几乎相同），并没说它在表现\n上'超过'了母亲；它超过的是普通狗，不是母亲。\n借用原文：the clone and her mother are almost identical genetically", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What is the scientists' purpose in cloning police dogs?", "options": ["A) To speed up investigation into criminal cases.", "B) To test the feasibility of cloning technology.", "C) To cut down training expenses.", "D) To reduce their training time."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （目的题） To reduce their training time.\n【定位】The ultimate goal of scientists is to produce clones of talented police dogs that can be trained\nin months instead of years.\n科学家的最终目标是培育出有天赋的警犬克隆体，使它们能在几个月内、而不是几年内完成\n训练。\n【信号】ultimate goal点出目的句。录音第二段 The ultimate goal of scientists is to produce\nclones 用 goal（目标）直接亮明目的，这正是目的题要等的那句话；其中 in months\ninstead of years（几个月而非几年）点出意图是缩短时间。\n【替换】trained in months instead of years ↔ reduce their training time\n几月而非几年=缩短时间\n【排除】A) 未提及\nTo speed up investigation into criminal cases. 借了第一段 crimes（犯罪）的余味，但录\n音讲的目的是缩短训练时间，从没说克隆是为了'加快案件调查'，属无中生有。\nB) 未提及\nTo test the feasibility of cloning technology. 听起来很像科研口吻，但录音里克隆的目的\n是缩短训练时间，并没说'是为了测试克隆技术是否可行'，是凭常识想当然添上的目的。\nC) 偷换概念\nTo cut down training expenses. 与正解只差一个词：录音说的是省'时间'（months\ninstead of years），不是省'费用'。原文里 costs（成本）出现在最后一句、说的是'技术成\n本高导致目标暂不可行'，是障碍、不是目的，被挪来当目的了。\n借用原文：this goal is not yet possible due to the current costs of the technology", "points": ["听力·长对话", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "Why does the news report say the scientists' goal is not yet possible?", "options": ["A) Cloning is too complicated a process.", "B) The technology is yet to be accepted.", "C) Cloning is ethically controversial.", "D) The technology is too expensive."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The technology is too expensive.\n【定位】However, this goal is not yet possible due to the current costs of the technology.\n然而，由于目前这项技术的成本问题，这个目标还无法实现。\n【信号】However转折加due to因果。录音第二段末句先用 However 转折（前面说目标美好、这\n里说还做不到），再用 due to the current costs of the technology 给出原因，转折+因\n果双信号叠在一句，正是 Why 题的标准答案句。\n【替换】the current costs of the technology ↔ The technology is too expensive.\n成本高=太贵\n【排除】A) 未提及\nCloning is too complicated a process. 听上去像个合理障碍，但录音明确把原因归到'成\n本'（costs），从没说过程'太复杂'，是凭常识猜的障碍。\nB) 偷换概念\nThe technology is yet to be accepted. 借用了录音 not yet possible（还不可行）里 yet\n的语感，但原文说做不到是因为贵，不是因为'还没被接受'，属偷换原因。\n借用原文：this goal is not yet possible due to the current costs of the technology\nC) 未提及\nCloning is ethically controversial. 是一般人一提'克隆'就联想到的伦理争议，但本则新闻\n只说障碍是成本，完全没提伦理，是被话题带偏的想当然选项。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "How did the man get to know about the book The Three Body Problem?", "options": ["A) He read it somewhere online.", "B) He heard about it from a coworker.", "C) He read an article reviewing it.", "D) He watched a TV series based on it."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） He heard about it from a coworker.\n【定位】A colleague mentioned the book, but I've been so busy writing my thesis that I haven't been\nable to read for pleasure in months.\n一个同事提到过这本书，但我一直忙着写论文，好几个月都没能读点闲书了。\n【信号】被问者的直接应答。上一句女士问起这书，男士开口第一句就是 A colleague mentioned\nthe book——对话题里答案就落在被问者的应答上，这句正面回答了「怎么知道的」，是\n答案句。\n【替换】A colleague mentioned the book ↔ He heard about it from a coworker\ncolleague 等于 coworker\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n女士开场说 did you see the article online，选项 He read it somewhere online. 把这个\nonline 搬过来，但「网上看到」的主语是女士的提问、说的是那篇文章，不是男士得知书的\n途径，属答非所问。\n借用原文：did you see the article online\nC) 偷换概念\n录音里的 the article online about the new TV series 是讲改编剧的报道，选项 He read\nan article reviewing it. 偷换成「读了一篇评论这本书的文章」，且读文章的是女士不是男\n士，既偷换内容又张冠李戴。\n借用原文：the article online about the new TV series\nD) 与原文相反\n录音通篇说剧还没看、甚至还没开播（When is the show supposed to start?），选项 He\nwatched a TV series based on it. 说他「看了改编剧」与事实相反，男士并没看过剧。\n借用原文：the new TV series based on the book", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the woman say she can't remember about the book's author?", "options": ["A) His publications.", "B) His first book.", "C) His address.", "D) His name."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） His name.\n【定位】It's written by a Chinese science fiction writer. I can't remember his name, but he's written\nthree books in all, and The Three Body Problem is the first in the series.\n它是一位中国科幻作家写的。我记不清他的名字了，不过他一共写了三本书，《三体》是这\n个系列的第一本。\n【信号】题干原词 can't remember 直接命中。题干问 can't remember，录音里女士原话就有 I\ncan't remember his name——can't remember 这个词组是直接信号，它后面紧跟的 his\nname 就是答案。\n【替换】I can't remember his name ↔ His name.\n原句直接点名 name\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n选项 His publications. 对应录音 he's written three books in all——但作者的著作女士记\n得清清楚楚（连写了三本、《三体》是第一本都说得出），她记不清的不是这个。\n借用原文：he's written three books in all\nB) 答非所问\n选项 His first book. 对应录音 The Three Body Problem is the first in the series，女士\n明确说得出第一本是《三体》，可见她记得，并非记不清的那一项。\n借用原文：The Three Body Problem is the first in the series\nC) 未提及\n选项 His address. 的 address 听起来像录音里某个词，但全篇女士只字未提作者的「住\n址」或「演讲」，这是无中生有。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the man have to do to finish his thesis?", "options": ["A) Collect a lot more data.", "B) Relax a bit less often.", "C) Clarify many new concepts.", "D) Read more reference books."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Collect a lot more data.\n【定位】I'm a bit overwhelmed with the amount of data I still need to collect to finish my thesis.\n为了完成论文，我还有大量数据要收集，这让我有点应付不过来。\n【信号】still need to + 题干同词 finish thesis。男士说 the amount of data I still need to\ncollect to finish my thesis，still need to（还得）是「不得不做」的信号词，且 finish my\nthesis 与题干 finish his thesis 撞词，这句正面回答了「为完成论文要做什么」。\n【替换】the amount of data I still need to collect ↔ Collect a lot more data.\nstill collect 等于 collect more\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n录音里男士说 But I still need to relax sometimes.——他强调「仍然需要放松」，选项\nRelax a bit less often. 却说「少放松一点」，把 relax 这个原词拿走后反着说，与原意相\n反。\n借用原文：I still need to relax sometimes\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Clarify many new concepts. 的 concepts 来自女士说书里有 some amazing\ntechnological and sociological concepts，那是夸书内容精彩，跟男士完成论文要做的事\n毫无关系。\n借用原文：some amazing technological and sociological concepts\nD) 未提及\n选项 Read more reference books. 蹭录音里反复出现的 read/book，但全篇说的「读」是\n读《三体》这本闲书，没有任何「读参考书做论文」的内容，属无中生有。\n借用原文：read the book first", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What will the man most probably do first after the conversation?", "options": ["A) Find out the show's most interesting episodes.", "B) Watch the series together with the woman.", "C) Get an e-copy of the book to read.", "D) Check to see when the show starts."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （推断题） Get an e-copy of the book to read.\n【定位】Well, maybe you could download an electronic copy of the book and try to read it before the\nshow starts.\n嗯，也许你可以下载一本电子版的书，试着在剧开播之前把它读了。\n【信号】建议句 + before the show starts 定先后。女士给出建议 maybe you could download\nan electronic copy of the book and try to read it before the show starts，建议句是落\n点；before the show starts（在开播前）明确点出「先读书」的次序，男士随即 That's a\ngood idea. 接受，故「先弄电子书读」被敲定。\n【替换】download an electronic copy of the book and try to read it ↔ Get an e-copy of the book to read\ndownload e-copy 等于 get e-copy\nbefore the show starts ↔ Get an e-copy of the book to read\n开播前读定下了先后\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Find out the show's most interesting episodes. 蹭了录音里 There are 24\nepisodes. 的 episodes，但女士只说有 24 集，从没提「找最精彩的几集」，属无中生有。\n借用原文：There are 24 episodes\nB) 答非所问\n男士确实说 maybe we can watch the series together，但那是「然后(and then)」「也\n许」之后的安排，要等读完书、剧开播。题目问 first（最先），先做的是读书不是看剧。\n借用原文：maybe we can watch the series together\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Check to see when the show starts. 来自男士问 When is the show supposed to\nstart?，但女士回 I can't remember exactly，且最终敲定的下一步是「先读书」，并没有\n让他去查开播时间。\n借用原文：When is the show supposed to start?", "points": ["听力·篇章", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "Why does the woman call the man?", "options": ["A) To check the prices of his farm produce.", "B) To ask the way to the Newcastle City Hall.", "C) To inquire about the vegetarian food festival.", "D) To seek the man's help with her work on the farm."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （目的题） To inquire about the vegetarian food festival.\n【定位】I have an inquiry to make. It's about the vegetarian food festival you are holding on the 19th\nof August at the Newcastle City Hall.\n我有件事想咨询。是关于你们将在8月19日于纽卡斯尔市政厅举办的素食美食节的。\n【信号】开场自报来意。女方一上来就说 I have an inquiry to make（我要咨询一件事），紧接着\n点明 It's about the vegetarian food festival——开场\"我打电话是为了……\"的自述就是目\n的题的答案句信号。\n【替换】an inquiry to make ↔ inquire about\n名词换动词同义\nthe vegetarian food festival ↔ the vegetarian food festival\n话题对象一致\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音里女方说自己 I grow organic vegetables on my farm，A借\"农产品\"做文章编出 To\ncheck the prices of his farm produce.\"查菜价\"，但全程没人提价格，属凭印象的臆造。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到 the Newcastle City Hall，B便顺势编成 To ask the way to the Newcastle\nCity Hall.\"问去市政厅的路\"。但市政厅只是节日地点，女方从没问路，这是\"听到地名就\n选\"的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：the Newcastle City Hall\nD) 偷换概念\n录音里 I grow organic vegetables on my farm 提到了农场，D便编成 To seek the man's\nhelp with her work on the farm.\"求男方帮她干农活\"。实际是她想在节上卖货，不是求人\n帮农活，方向反了。\n借用原文：I grow organic vegetables on my farm", "points": ["听力·篇章", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What is the man still looking for?", "options": ["A) Bakers.", "B) Vendors.", "C) Vegetarians.", "D) Organisers."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Vendors.\n【定位】It says on your website that you are still looking for vendors, and I grow organic vegetables\non my farm, as well as doing my own home baking.\n你们网站上说你们还在找摊主，而我在自家农场种有机蔬菜，也自己在家做烘焙。\n【信号】still looking for直接给出招募对象。女方转述网站内容 you are still looking for\nvendors，still looking for 正是\"还在招\"的信号，紧跟其后的 vendors 就是答案对象。\n【替换】vendors ↔ Vendors.\n原词对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n女方说自己 doing my own home baking（在家做烘焙），A便顺手把她说成 Bakers.\"面\n包师\"。但她只是会烘焙的摊主之一，男方招的是\"摊主\"不是\"面包师\"，属听到 baking 就联\n想的误选。\n借用原文：doing my own home baking\nC) 原词陷阱\n节日叫 vegetarian food festival，C便取 Vegetarians.\"素食者\"充数。但\"素食\"是节日主\n题，不是男方招募的人群，男方招的是卖货的摊主。\n借用原文：vegetarian food festival\nD) 张冠李戴\n男方一方正是办活动的组织者，D取 Organisers.\"组织者\"反而是他们自己的身份，不是\"还\n在找\"的对象，角色装反了。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the man say they are getting close to?", "options": ["A) The issuing of certificates to vendors.", "B) The completion of the baking task.", "C) The festival they are organising.", "D) The deadline for application."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The deadline for application.\n【定位】That's exactly the type of thing we are looking for. We're getting close to the\ndeadline, however.\n这正是我们想要的那种摊位。不过，我们快到截止日期了。\n【信号】getting close to紧跟落点。男方说 We're getting close to the deadline，getting close\nto 这一\"临近\"信号后面紧跟 the deadline，即他们正逼近的那件事。\n【替换】the deadline ↔ The deadline for application.\n概括补全为完整短语\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 all your certificates 和 certificates will be checked 之说，A拼成 The issuing\nof certificates to vendors.\"给摊主发证书\"。但证书是报名时要备齐、会被审核，录音并没\n说\"快到发证书\"，且\"发证\"也非原文之意，是借证书原词的拼凑。\n借用原文：all your certificates\nB) 原词陷阱\n女方提过 home baking，B据此编成 The completion of the baking task.\"烘焙任务完\n成\"。但录音从没说\"快完成烘焙\"，且\"close to\"接的是 deadline 不是 baking。\n借用原文：home baking\nC) 答非所问\nThe festival they are organising. 取了贯穿全程的话题 the vegetarian food festival，\n但\"快到美食节\"并不是男方所说，他说的是快到截止日。话题对、答非所问。\n借用原文：the vegetarian food festival", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the man finally ask the woman to remember?", "options": ["A) The closing date of submission.", "B) The website of his company.", "C) The details of the ceremony.", "D) The organiser's address."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The closing date of submission.\n【定位】Remember that the closing date is next Tuesday, the 28th of June.\n记住，截止日期是下周二，也就是6月28日。\n【信号】结尾Remember领出叮嘱。男方在对话末尾说 Remember that the closing date is next\nTuesday，Remember that 是\"特意叮嘱\"的信号，其后的 the closing date 即他要她记住\n的事。\n【替换】the closing date ↔ The closing date of submission.\n补出提交语境\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音前面女方提过 It says on your website，B便取 The website of his company.\"他公司\n的网址\"。但网址出现在对话前段、且不是男方让她记住的事，Remember 点的是截止日不\n是网址。\n借用原文：It says on your website\nC) 未提及\nThe details of the ceremony.\"典礼细节\"录音里压根没出现过 ceremony 这个事，是凭空\n捏造的干扰。\nD) 原词陷阱\n男方确实在同段报了地址 Please address it to the Organic Organization，D据此取 The\norganiser's address.。但地址是应女方\"give me your details\"的请求顺带给的，被\nRemember 单独叮嘱的是\"截止日\"，不是地址，属\"听到地址就选\"的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：Please address it to the Organic Organization", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "Why has wild camping become popular in the UK?", "options": ["A) Most scenic sites have been closed.", "B) Access to official campsites is limited.", "C) Health experts advise going outdoors.", "D) People have more time during the summer."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Access to official campsites is limited.\n【定位】In part, this reflects the fact that official campsites have been wholly or partially closed, or are\noverflowing in a summer when fewer people are going abroad.\n在一定程度上，这反映出一个事实：正规营地已经全部或部分关闭，或者在出国人数减少的\n这个夏天人满为患。\n【信号】原因信号 this reflects the fact that。this reflects the fact that 是典型的解释原因句\n式，后面紧跟的 official campsites have been wholly or partially closed, or are\noverflowing 就是\"野营为何流行\"的直接答案。这种\"反映出/源于\"的句子，正是原因题该锁\n定的答案句。\n【替换】official campsites have been wholly or partially closed, or are overflowing ↔\nAccess to official campsites is limited.\n\"关闭/人满\"概括为\"使用受限\"\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n录音关闭的是 official campsites（正规露营营地），不是 scenic sites（风景景点）。A\n把\"营地\"偷换成\"景点\"，听着相似却不是同一回事。\n借用原文：official campsites have been wholly or partially closed\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音确实提到 a desire for going outdoors（向往户外），C 也写 going outdoors，原词\n重合很诱人。但录音说的是\"普通人的一种向往\"，从没说是 Health experts（健康专家）建\n议的；且这只是次要补充原因（it may also be），不是题干所问的主因。听到 going\noutdoors 就选 C，正中原词陷阱。\n借用原文：an expression of a desire for going outdoors\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里 in a summer（夏天）确实出现，让 D 的 during the summer 显得耳熟。但录音\n从未说人们\"有更多时间（more time）\"，这是凭空添加的原因。盗了\"夏天\"一个词，就编\n出一个录音没讲的理由。\n借用原文：in a summer when fewer people are going abroad", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "Why is camping banned for part of the summer in public parks?", "options": ["A) It is strongly opposed by nearby residents.", "B) It leads to much waste of public money.", "C) It has caused environmental concerns.", "D) It has created conflicts among campers."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It has caused environmental concerns.\n【定位】Camping in public parks has now been banned for August and the early part of September\nbecause campers dump litter, human waste, and even their tents on the grassland.\n公园露营如今已在八月和九月上旬被禁，因为露营者把垃圾、人体排泄物、甚至帐篷都丢弃\n在草地上。\n【信号】因果信号 because。because 直接引出禁露营的原因：campers dump litter, human\nwaste, and even their tents on the grassland。because 后面的内容就是答案句的核心，\n因果题听到 because 就该锁定。\n【替换】campers dump litter, human waste, and even their tents on the grassland ↔\nIt has caused environmental concerns.\n乱丢垃圾的具体行为概括为环境问题\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音只说禁露营是因乱丢垃圾，全程没提 nearby residents（附近居民）反对。这是录音根\n本没说的内容，凭常识\"居民会嫌吵\"补上去就错了。\nB) 未提及\n录音讲的是垃圾污染草地，从没提 waste of public money（浪费公共资金）。这是凭空添\n加的后果，与禁露营的真实原因无关。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音反复出现 campers（露营者），D 也写 campers，原词重合很诱人。但录音说的是露\n营者\"破坏环境\"，从没说他们\"彼此起冲突（conflicts among campers）\"。听到 campers\n就选 D，正中原词陷阱。\n借用原文：campers dump litter, human waste, and even their tents on the grassland", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does the speaker suggest campers do?", "options": ["A) Look for open land in Scotland.", "B) Leave no trace of their camping.", "C) Avoid getting close to wilderness.", "D) Ask for permission from authorities."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Leave no trace of their camping.\n【定位】It would be common sense for people to use small tents and leave no trace of their visit.\n人们使用小帐篷、不留下到访的任何痕迹，是合乎常理的做法。\n【信号】建议信号 It would be common sense for...to。It would be common sense for\npeople to 是劝告做法的句式，后面 leave no trace of their visit 就是说话人给露营者的核\n心建议。建议题该锁定这类\"应当/合乎常理去做\"的句子。\n【替换】leave no trace of their visit ↔ Leave no trace of their camping.\nvisit换成camping，近乎同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音提到 especially outside Scotland（尤其在苏格兰以外），A 也写 Scotland，原词重\n合很诱人。但录音是说\"在苏格兰以外法律更严、要请求许可\"，根本没建议人们\"去苏格兰找\n开放土地\"。听到 Scotland 就选 A，正中原词陷阱。\n借用原文：to camp from landowners, especially outside Scotland, where the law is far\nmore restrictive\nC) 与原文相反\n录音出现 close to wilderness（靠近荒野），C 也写 wilderness，但录音是说人们\"被这块\n靠近荒野的土地吸引、有责任维护它\"，绝不是叫人 Avoid getting close to wilderness\n（避开荒野）。意思被反着说了，盗了原词却背道而驰。\n借用原文：They have been attracted by a patch of land this close to wilderness\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音确说要 ask for permission to camp from landowners（向地主请求许可），D 写\nAsk for permission 原词重合很像。但录音的对象是 landowners（土地所有者），D 却换\n成了 authorities（官方当局），把请求对象偷换了。听到 ask for permission 就选 D，正\n中原词陷阱。\n借用原文：wild campers need to ask for permission to camp from landowners", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the passage say about pink dolphins?", "options": ["A) They outcompete mythical creatures.", "B) They usually mind their own business.", "C) They truly exist in the Amazon region.", "D) They resemble alarmingly large snakes."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They truly exist in the Amazon region.\n【定位】While this may seem like a mythical creature, pink dolphins do exist in the Amazon region.\n虽然这看上去像是神话中的生物，但粉色海豚确实存在于亚马逊地区。\n【信号】转折 while + 强调 do。信号有两层：让步转折 While this may seem like a mythical\ncreature 先抑（看似神话），转折后才是真意；再加强调助动词 pink dolphins do exist 中\n的 do，把存在性钉死。转折之后、带强调语气的那句正是答案句。\n【替换】pink dolphins do exist in the Amazon region ↔ They truly exist in the Amazon region.\ndo exist 同义改写为 truly exist\n【排除】D) 原词陷阱\n录音里 alarmingly large snakes 说的是船客在河上提防的大蛇，是另一种动物，根本不是\n在说海豚像蛇。选项 They resemble alarmingly large snakes. 原封盗用 alarmingly large\nsnakes 这个词组，把蛇安到海豚头上，听到原词就想选正中下怀。\n借用原文：calmly keeping an eye out for alarmingly large snakes\nB)\n录音 minding your own business 描述的是划船的你在专心赶路，不是海豚的习性。选项\nThey usually mind their own business. 把这句原话改成海豚不爱招惹别人，主语偷换成\n海豚，且原文从未说海豚的脾性，属答非所问。\n借用原文：minding your own business\nA)\n录音只说海豚 may seem like a mythical creature（看似神话生物），并未把海豚和神话\n生物放在一起比高下。选项 They outcompete mythical creatures. 凭一个 mythical\ncreature 编出胜过神话生物的说法，原文无此意，属过度推断。\n借用原文：While this may seem like a mythical creature", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What is the unusual coloring of pink dolphins believed to originate in?", "options": ["A) Scar tissue from dolphins' fighting.", "B) Skin infection from water pollution.", "C) Unhealed wounds from snake bites.", "D) Swimming along in seasonal floods."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Scar tissue from dolphins' fighting.\n【定位】Their unusual coloring is believed to be the result of scar tissue from dolphin fights—whether\nplayful fighting or a serious bid for a mate.\n它们这种奇特的颜色，被认为是海豚打斗留下的疤痕组织造成的——无论是嬉戏打闹还是为\n争夺配偶的认真争斗。\n【信号】因果 the result of。答案信号是因果短语 is believed to be the result of，它后面接的正\n是颜色的来源。题干问 originate in，原文用 result of 应答，听到 result of 立刻锁定其后\n内容。\n【替换】scar tissue from dolphin fights ↔ Scar tissue from dolphins' fighting.\nfights 改写为 fighting\n【排除】D) 原词陷阱\n选项 Swimming along in seasonal floods. 里的 seasonal floods 来自第 2 段讲栖息地的\ntemporary lakes caused by seasonal flooding，与颜色来源无关。它盗用了原文出现过\n的 seasonal flood 制造耳熟，但答的不是本题问的成因。\n借用原文：temporary lakes caused by seasonal flooding\nB) 未提及\n选项 Skin infection from water pollution. 说颜色来自水污染引起的皮肤感染，可原文讲\n来源时只字未提 pollution 或 infection，是凭空捏造的成因，属未提及。\nC)\n选项 Unhealed wounds from snake bites. 把成因说成蛇咬的未愈伤口，借第 1 段的\nsnakes 联想造势，但原文讲颜色来源从未说被蛇咬，属未提及加错接。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What has become of the Amazon wetland system since 2018?", "options": ["A) It has been shrinking at an astonishing pace.", "B) It has been placed under international protection.", "C) It has been appealing to both freshwater and sea dolphins.", "D) It has been abandoned as a battleground for male dolphins."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It has been placed under international protection.\n【定位】The Amazon wetland system, fed by the Amazon River, is a crucial place for pink dolphins to\nbreed, and since 2018 has been granted internationally protected status.\n由亚马逊河滋养的亚马逊湿地系统，是粉色海豚繁殖的关键之地，并自 2018 年起被授予国\n际保护地位。\n【信号】时间锚 since 2018。答案信号是时间状语 since 2018，它与题干的 since 2018 完全对\n应，后面紧跟的 has been granted internationally protected status 就是答案。听到年份\n与题干同步，立即抓其后内容。\n【替换】has been granted internationally protected status ↔\nIt has been placed under international protection.\ngranted protected status 改写为 placed under protection\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\n选项 It has been appealing to both freshwater and sea dolphins. 里的 freshwater 取自\n第 2 段 this freshwater dolphin，且原文只有淡水海豚、根本没提海水海豚或湿地吸引两\n类海豚，属盗用原词加未提及。\n借用原文：this freshwater dolphin is sometimes shockingly pink\nA)\n选项 It has been shrinking at an astonishing pace. 说湿地在惊人缩小，方向与原文相反\n——原文说它自 2018 年起 granted internationally protected status，是受到保护、向\n好，绝非缩小。这正是预读时提醒过的正反对立陷阱。\n借用原文：since 2018 has been granted internationally protected status\nD)\n选项 It has been abandoned as a battleground for male dolphins. 借第 3 段 dolphin\nfights 与雄性海豚的字眼，编出湿地被弃为雄海豚战场的说法，可原文恰说湿地是繁殖关键\n地、被保护，从未说被弃用，属张冠李戴加相反。\n借用原文：scar tissue from dolphin fights", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What do we learn from a new survey by Merrill Lynch/Age Wave?", "options": ["A) About 58% of young adults call parental support the new normal.", "B) Most adult children enjoy increasing sources of financial support.", "C) A full 70% of the young adults cannot afford to buy a car by themselves.", "D) Most early adults cannot sustain their lifestyles without parental support."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Most early adults cannot sustain their lifestyles without parental support.\n【定位】In a new Merrill Lynch/Age Wave survey, a full 70% of the early adults said they received\nfinancial support from their parents in the past year and 58% said they couldn't afford their\ncurrent lifestyles without it.\n在美林/年龄浪潮的一项新调查中，整整 70% 的年轻成年人表示，过去一年里他们得到了父\n母的经济资助，而 58% 的人表示，没有这份资助他们就负担不起当前的生活方式。\n【信号】开篇报数据即调查发现落点。录音一开篇就给出 a full 70% of the early adults said they\nreceived financial support 与 58% said they couldn't afford their current lifestyles\nwithout it 两组数据，篇章独白\"开头点题报核心发现\"，这两句就是承载答案的句子，关键\n在把每个数字拴到它说明的那件事。\n【替换】58% said they couldn't afford their current lifestyles without it ↔\nMost early adults cannot sustain their lifestyles without parental support\n同义改写且 58% 即\"多数\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项把 58% 与 the new normal 拼在一起，但原文里 58% 对应的是\"撑不起生活\"，而 the\nnew normal 是 CEO 个人的评价、根本没说\"58% 的人这么叫它\"，属盗用真实数字后乱配\n对象。\n借用原文：58% said they couldn't afford their current lifestyles without it\nB) 未提及\nenjoy increasing sources of financial support 凭空加了\"资助来源在增加\"且带正面色彩\nenjoy，原文只列了 cell phone plans, food, school costs and car expenses 几类资助、\n从未说它们在\"增多\"，更没说年轻人\"乐享\"，是无中生有式改写。\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项把真实的 70% 接到 cannot afford to buy a car by themselves 上，但原文 70% 说\n的是 received financial support from their parents，\"车\"只是 car expenses 作为资助类\n别被顺带提到，并没有\"70% 的人买不起车\"这回事，是典型的数字配错对象。\n借用原文：a full 70% of the early adults said they received financial support from their parents", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "Why did most young adults in the survey say financial support to children aged 25 to 34 is a bad thing?", "options": ["A) It renders them dependent.", "B) It causes them to lose dignity.", "C) It makes them mentally immature.", "D) It hinders them from getting ahead."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It renders them dependent.\n【定位】But 64% of the young adults surveyed said parents' financial support to children age 25 to 34\nis \"a bad thing\", because it makes those kids dependent.\n但是，64% 的受访年轻人表示，父母对 25 至 34 岁子女的经济资助是\"一件坏事\"，因为它\n让这些孩子变得依赖。\n【信号】因果词 because 后即答案。本句用 because it makes those kids dependent 直接给出理\n由，because 是最硬的因果信号，紧跟其后的 makes those kids dependent 就是答案落\n点，前半句只是在陈述\"是坏事\"这一立场。\n【替换】it makes those kids dependent ↔ It renders them dependent\nmake 与 render 同义、them 回指 kids\n【排除】B) 未提及\nIt causes them to lose dignity. 听上去也像\"坏处\"，但原文给的理由只有\"变依赖\"，从未提\n到 dignity（尊严），是顺着\"坏事\"语境编出的合理化干扰。\nC) 过度推断\nIt makes them mentally immature. 把\"依赖\"过度引申成\"心智不成熟\"，原文只说\ndependent、没说 mentally immature，属于在原意上加码的推断，超出文本。\nD) 与原文相反\nIt hinders them from getting ahead. 盗用了原文的 get ahead，但原文 helps the adult\nchildren get ahead 是讲资助 18-24 岁\"好处\"时说的、且是\"helps（帮助）出人头地\"，选\n项却反成\"hinders（阻碍）\"并安到\"坏事\"这边，既偷原词又正话反说、还把对象张冠李戴。\n借用原文：the remaining 71% think that assistance \"helps the adult children get ahead\"", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What did the respondents in the survey say regarding financial independence?", "options": ["A) It challenges one's willpower.", "B) It results from education.", "C) It calls for due assistance.", "D) It defines adulthood."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It defines adulthood.\n【定位】In fact, the respondents said, financial independence defines adulthood.\n事实上，受访者表示，经济独立定义了成年。\n【信号】the respondents said 后的直接定性。题干问受访者说了什么，原文 the respondents\nsaid, financial independence defines adulthood 用\"受访者表示\"直接引出他们的定性，\n主语应答方明确、又含\"下定义\"的 defines，正是承载答案的句子。\n【替换】financial independence defines adulthood ↔ It defines adulthood\nIt 回指经济独立、defines 原词保留\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt challenges one's willpower. 借了原文 challenged by 的影子，但原文 challenged 出现\n在 CEO 转述\"经济独立让年轻人感到挣扎、受挑战\"处，谈的是\"被它难住\"而非\"它考验意志\n力\"，且这不是题干要的\"受访者对经济独立的定性\"。\n借用原文：Financial independence is something they were struggling with and challenged by\nB) 未提及\nIt results from education. 把\"经济独立\"归因于教育，原文从未谈两者的来源关系；\neducation 一词在原文压根没出现，是凭空捏造的因果。\nC) 与原文相反\nIt calls for due assistance.（需要适当帮助）与受访者的态度正好相反——他们认为 by\n25, younger adults ought to be financially independent，强调的是\"应当独立\"，而\n非\"需要援助\"，方向反了。\n借用原文：by 25, younger adults ought to be financially independent", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What is one big reason young adults are struggling?", "options": ["A) Current lifestyles.", "B) Poor budgeting.", "C) College loans.", "D) Emergency expenses."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） College loans.\n【定位】One big reason they're struggling is attributed to college loans, of which the average amounts\nto $37,000.\n他们之所以挣扎，一个大原因被归结为助学贷款，其平均金额高达 37000 美元。\n【信号】one big reason… attributed to 双因果信号。本句 One big reason they're struggling\nis attributed to college loans 与题干几乎同形，one big reason 与 attributed to 是叠加\n的因果信号，attributed to 后点出的 college loans 就是答案落点。\n【替换】attributed to college loans ↔ College loans\n答案原词直现、近乎照搬\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nCurrent lifestyles 盗用了第 1 段 current lifestyles，但那是讲\"没资助就撑不起当前生\n活\"时说的，与本题问的\"挣扎的原因\"无关，纯粹靠耳熟设饵。\n借用原文：58% said they couldn't afford their current lifestyles without it\nB) 未提及\nPoor budgeting（不善理财）像是一个合情合理的\"挣扎原因\"，但原文归因明确指向\ncollege loans，从未提及预算或理财问题，是凭常识脑补的干扰。\nD) 未提及\nEmergency expenses（应急开销）与正确项 college loans 同属\"花钱\"类、极易混淆，但\n原文归因只点了助学贷款，根本没提 emergency expenses，是同类干扰。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constitutes", "B) contradictory", "C) decision", "D) detailed", "E) difference", "F) dipping", "G) distinction", "H) falling", "I) involves", "J) moderate", "K) notified", "L) partly", "M) required", "N) traditionally", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) detailed\n形容词\n【词性槽】have ___, evidence-backed guidelines，修饰 guidelines\n【依据】与 evidence-backed 并列修饰\"指南\"，最贴的是\"详细的\"\nguidelines 的常见修饰是\"详细的、有循证支撑的\"——detailed, evidence-backed\nguidelines 正是常见地道搭配；且与下文 such guidelines do not yet exist for exercise\nand mood 形成\"运动健身的指南详尽，但情绪方向还没有\"的对比。\n【竞争词】B) contradictory \"相互矛盾的指南\"逻辑不通\ncontradictory 表\"自相矛盾的\"，作者要赞大多数国家有靠谱的指南，不会说\"自相矛盾\"。\nJ) moderate moderate 修饰\"强度\"而非\"指南\"\nmoderate 在本文专指\"中等强度\"（见 35 题），用来修饰 guidelines 既不搭配也无意义。\nO) vigorous vigorous 修饰\"运动\"，不修饰\"指南\"\nvigorous 常与 exercise/activity 搭配表\"剧烈的\"，无法形容指南文本。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constitutes", "B) contradictory", "C) decision", "D) detailed", "E) difference", "F) dipping", "G) distinction", "H) falling", "I) involves", "J) moderate", "K) notified", "L) partly", "M) required", "N) traditionally", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) required\n动词\n【词性槽】the type and intensity of exercise ___ for various…，后置定语动词\n【依据】be required for = 为……所必需\n原句要表达\"为获得各种身体健康益处所需要的运动种类与强度\"——guidelines on the\ntype and intensity of exercise required for…physical health benefits，required for 与\n\"for various physical health benefits\" 自然搭配。\n【竞争词】K) notified notify 的对象是人，不是\"运动\"\nnotify 表\"通知（某人）\"，主语通常是\"通知的内容\"，而\"运动被通知\"语义荒谬；且 notified for\n也非地道搭配，应是 notified of/about。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constitutes", "B) contradictory", "C) decision", "D) detailed", "E) difference", "F) dipping", "G) distinction", "H) falling", "I) involves", "J) moderate", "K) notified", "L) partly", "M) required", "N) traditionally", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) partly\n副词\n【词性槽】be 动词 + ___ + due to，空处修饰整个表语\n【依据】下文转折\"However\"暗示\"部分原因\"而非全部\n空后是 due to a lack of necessary evidence. However, a new systematic review brings\nus usefully up to-date on the current findings in this area.，\"However\" 表明证据并非\n全无、综述已带来更新，所以缺乏证据只是\"部分\"原因，partly 与下文逻辑天然吻合。\n【竞争词】N) traditionally \"传统上\"与上文\"目前还没有指南\"矛盾\n上文 such guidelines do not yet exist for exercise and mood 讲的是\"目前\"的状况，\ntraditionally 意为\"传统上、历来\"，时间指向错位；况且\"传统上由于缺证据\"读起来也不符合科\n学综述的客观语气。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 N) —— traditionally 与\"due to\"看似自然，但与下文 However 矛\n盾。traditionally 单看本句\"传统上是由于缺乏证据\"也成立，副词位、句法都对；防坑：副\n词空务必读到下一句的逻辑词——However, a new systematic review brings us usefully\nup to-date 已部分推翻\"缺证据\"的说法，所以只能是\"部分原因\"partly。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constitutes", "B) contradictory", "C) decision", "D) detailed", "E) difference", "F) dipping", "G) distinction", "H) falling", "I) involves", "J) moderate", "K) notified", "L) partly", "M) required", "N) traditionally", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) dipping\n动词\n【词性槽】介词 Before 后 + ___ into，需动名词\n【依据】dip into = 涉足、稍稍探讨某话题\n下文 Before __(29)__ into some of the key take-aways, an important __(30)__ made in\nthe review is between aerobic exercise and anaerobic. 是过渡句——\"在涉足/简略谈及\n这些要点之前，先要……\"。dip into 正是\"稍微进入、涉足\"的固定搭配，与\"key take-\naways（关键要点）\"搭配自然，且贴合综述类文章的口吻。\n【竞争词】H) falling fall into 意为\"陷入/掉入\"，多含被动或贬义\nfall into 常表\"陷入（困境/陷阱/分类）\"，逻辑上不是作者主动\"探讨\"key take-aways，而是\"掉\n进去\"，与作者掌控话题节奏的语气不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constitutes", "B) contradictory", "C) decision", "D) detailed", "E) difference", "F) dipping", "G) distinction", "H) falling", "I) involves", "J) moderate", "K) notified", "L) partly", "M) required", "N) traditionally", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) distinction\n名词\n【词性槽】an important ___ made…between A and B\n【依据】make a distinction between A and B = 区分 A 与 B\n空后明确是 between aerobic exercise and anaerobic——在有氧与无氧之间作\"区分\"。\nmake a distinction between 是地道搭配，且下文整段都在分别解释两类运动的差别，正\n是\"区分\"在展开。\n【竞争词】C) decision make a decision 不与 between A and B 这样接\"两类区分\"\nmake a decision 一般搭 about/on，且表\"在 A 与 B 之间作决定\"时是二选一，本文并不是\n要\"在有氧和无氧之间二选一\"，而是要把它们\"区分开\"以便分别讨论。语义指向不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constitutes", "B) contradictory", "C) decision", "D) detailed", "E) difference", "F) dipping", "G) distinction", "H) falling", "I) involves", "J) moderate", "K) notified", "L) partly", "M) required", "N) traditionally", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) involves\n动词\n【词性槽】The former ___ such things as…，主语后缺谓语\n【依据】involves such things as = 包括诸如……这样的事物\ninvolve + such things as 是地道搭配，表\"包括、涉及（举例）\"。文中要表达\"前者（有氧\n运动）包括走路、慢跑、骑车这样的项目\"，involve 既符合搭配又符合\"举例罗列\"的语义。\n【竞争词】A) constitutes constitute 表\"构成整体\"，与\"列举若干例\"不符\nconstitute 的逻辑是\"A、B、C 这些东西构成 X\"或\"X 由 A、B、C 构成\"——把列举对象当成 X\n的全部组分。但本句只是举\"诸如\"走路、慢跑、骑车这类的例子，并非穷举构成有氧运动的全部\n内容，constitute 语气过死。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constitutes", "B) contradictory", "C) decision", "D) detailed", "E) difference", "F) dipping", "G) distinction", "H) falling", "I) involves", "J) moderate", "K) notified", "L) partly", "M) required", "N) traditionally", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) vigorous\n形容词\n【词性槽】of such ___ intensity that…，定语修饰 intensity\n【依据】举例\"举重\"+结果\"来不及用氧\"=高强度\n原句 anaerobic exercise—such as lifting heavy weights—is of such __(32)__ intensity\nthat your body does not have time to use oxygen to create energy：以\"举重\"为例，\n强度大到身体来不及用氧——只有\"剧烈的\"vigorous 能既匹配举例又匹配结果。\n【竞争词】B) contradictory contradictory 不修饰\"强度\"\ncontradictory 用于\"陈述/观点/结果\"等，搭配 intensity 不成立；且语义上\"自相矛盾的强度\"也\n不解释\"来不及用氧\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constitutes", "B) contradictory", "C) decision", "D) detailed", "E) difference", "F) dipping", "G) distinction", "H) falling", "I) involves", "J) moderate", "K) notified", "L) partly", "M) required", "N) traditionally", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) contradictory\n形容词\n【词性槽】found ___ results，作 results 的前置定语\n【依据】下文\"有的偏高、有的偏低、还有的无差别\"=互相矛盾\n紧接的具体说明 Some favoured higher intensity, others low, while seven studies\nfound that intensity made no __(34)__ to mood benefits. 一句话三种立场互相冲突——\n这正是 contradictory results\"相互矛盾的研究结果\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constitutes", "B) contradictory", "C) decision", "D) detailed", "E) difference", "F) dipping", "G) distinction", "H) falling", "I) involves", "J) moderate", "K) notified", "L) partly", "M) required", "N) traditionally", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) difference\n名词\n【词性槽】made no ___ to，固定搭配 make a difference\n【依据】make no difference to = 对……毫无影响\n上文 Some favoured higher intensity, others low, while seven studies found that\nintensity made no __(34)__ to mood benefits.\"有些研究偏高强度、有些偏低、还有七项\n发现强度对情绪益处无影响\"——difference 正好表达\"无差别/无影响\"的语义。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constitutes", "B) contradictory", "C) decision", "D) detailed", "E) difference", "F) dipping", "G) distinction", "H) falling", "I) involves", "J) moderate", "K) notified", "L) partly", "M) required", "N) traditionally", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) moderate\n形容词\n【词性槽】is ___ intensity，作 intensity 的定语\n【依据】下文\"低太闷、高太难受\"夹出中间值\n紧接的解释 perhaps because low intensity is too dull while high intensity is too\nunpleasant. 给了一道送分题——既排斥 low（太枯燥）又排斥 high（太难受），那\"最佳\n选择\"自然是介于两者之间的 moderate（中等）。\n【竞争词】O) vigorous vigorous = 剧烈，与\"高强度太 unpleasant\"矛盾\nvigorous intensity 等同于 high intensity，而下文直接否定了 high intensity，故 vigorous 反\n而是命题人最爱埋的诱惑项。\nB) contradictory \"自相矛盾的强度\"不通\ncontradictory 修饰\"结论/说法\"，不修饰\"强度\"，搭配错位、语义也无法解读。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 O) —— vigorous 与 intensity 搭配地道，最易误选。vigorous 修\n饰 intensity 在搭配上完全成立（vigorous intensity 与 vigorous exercise 都常见），不读\n后半句的考生很容易顺手选它；防坑：读完 high intensity is too unpleasant 后再回头\n——高强度被否，vigorous=高强度，必排除。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Some people view socializing at the workplace as a chance to develop personal relationships.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】把职场社交当作发展私人关系的机会\n【定位】Another theory Friedman considers is that, in an era of ever fewer settings that provide\neffective opportunities for personal connections and relationships, people may place more\nvalue on the socializing that happens at work.\n弗里德曼考察的另一种理论是：在一个能提供有效私人联结与关系机会的场合越来越少的时\n代，人们可能更看重发生在工作场所的社交。\n【改写】socializing at the workplace ↔ the socializing that happens at work\n同义替换\na chance to develop personal relationships ↔\neffective opportunities for personal connections and relationships\n上位概括 + 名词转动词", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "As ordinary American workers' average hourly pay had decreased despite increasing productivity, they had to work just as many hours as before to keep their living standards.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】时薪下降但生产率上升、为维持生活水平不得不维持工时\n【定位】Thus, though American incomes may have gone up since 1973, the amount that American\nworkers can actually buy with their money has gone down. For most Americans, then, the\nmagic of increasing productivity stopped working around 1973. Thus, they had to keep\nworking just as much in order to maintain their standard of living.\n因此，尽管 1973 年以来美国人的收入或许有所增加，但美国工人实际能用这些钱买到的东\n西却变少了。对大多数美国人而言，生产率上升的魔力大约在 1973 年前后就不再奏效，所\n以为了维持生活水平，他们不得不一如既往地继续工作。\n【改写】ordinary American workers' average hourly pay had decreased ↔\nthe average hourly wage for ordinary workers had fallen 5 percent from the 1973 level in\nterms of actual purchasing power\n数字弱化 + 同义替换\ndespite increasing productivity ↔\nthe magic of increasing productivity stopped working around 1973\n尽管/即便的逻辑等值\nthey had to work just as many hours as before to keep their living standards ↔\nthey had to keep working just as much in order to maintain their standard of living\n同义词替换 + 句式同构\n【辨邻】 G G 段虽然也涉及生产率与生活水平，但说的是 output by 2029 is likely to be around", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "American workers' average weekly working time has not changed for nearly half a century.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】周工时近半个世纪没变\n【定位】Instead of continuing to decline, the duration of the working week remained stable. It has\nstayed at just below 40 hours for nearly five decades.\n周工时没有继续下降，而是保持稳定，在略低于 40 小时的水平上保持了将近五十年。\n【改写】American workers' average weekly working time ↔ the duration of the working week\n同义替换 + 默认主语\nhas not changed for nearly half a century ↔\nremained stable 与 It has stayed at just below 40 hours for nearly five decades\n否定式同义改写 + 数字单位换算\n【辨邻】 A A 段虽然也讲美国周工时，但描述的是 by 1970, the number of hours Americans\nworked on average had fallen to slightly less than 39 这一段下降史实，方向是下\n降而不是 has not changed for nearly half a century 的停滞，时间段也不同。\nby 1970, the number of hours Americans worked on average had fallen to slightly less than 39", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Friedman believes inequality in the U.S. largely explains why increasing productivity has not resulted in reduced working hours.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】弗里德曼用不平等解释生产率上升却没换来更少工时\n【定位】A third possibility proves more convincing for Friedman. That is: American inequality means\nthat the gains of increasing productivity are not widely shared by everyone. In other words,\nmost Americans are too poor to work less.\n第三种可能性对弗里德曼来说更有说服力——美国的不平等意味着生产率上升的收益并没有\n被所有人广泛分享；换句话说，大多数美国人穷得没办法少工作。\n【改写】Friedman believes inequality in the U.S. largely explains why increasing productivity has not\nresulted in reduced working hours\n↔ A third possibility proves more convincing for Friedman\n态度强度同义\ninequality in the U.S. ↔ American inequality\n形容词与介词短语互换\nwhy increasing productivity has not resulted in reduced working hours ↔\nthe gains of increasing productivity are not widely shared by everyone 与 most Americans are\ntoo poor to work less\n因果链改写 + 结果同义\n【辨邻】 D D 段同样涉及弗里德曼的解释体系，但讨论的是 people just never feel materially\nsatisfied 这种物欲假说，弗里德曼对它的态度是 far from sufficient，与陈述句的\ninequality 主张与 Friedman believes 的强度都不一致。\npeople just never feel materially satisfied, always wanting more money to buy the next new thing\nE E 段讨论的是 people may place more value on the socializing that happens at\nwork 这一社交价值假说，弗里德曼的态度是 the evidence for this theory is far\nfrom conclusive，与陈述句要的 inequality 不平等假说不是同一维度。\npeople may place more value on the socializing that happens at work", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Many economists assume people's thirst for material things has prevented them from enjoying more leisure time.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】许多经济学家认为对物质的渴望挡住了人享受闲暇\n【定位】Perhaps people just never feel materially satisfied, always wanting more money to buy the\nnext new thing. This is a theory that appeals to many economists.\n也许是人在物质上从不会感到满足，总想要更多的钱去买下一件新东西。这是一种让许多经\n济学家信服的理论。\n【改写】Many economists assume ↔ This is a theory that appeals to many economists\n持有理论 = 认为\npeople's thirst for material things ↔\npeople just never feel materially satisfied, always wanting more money to buy the next new\nthing\n名词化概括\nprevented them from enjoying more leisure time ↔\nwhy that increased productivity has not translated into increased leisure time\n因果反向同义\n【辨邻】 E E 段虽然也是弗里德曼考察的另一种解释，但观点是 people may place more value\non the socializing that happens at work，关键词是社交不是物质，不符合陈述句的\nthirst for material things。\npeople may place more value on the socializing that happens at work\nF F 段的核心解释是 American inequality means that the gains of increasing\nproductivity are not widely shared by everyone，归因落在不平等，与 thirst for\nmaterial things 的物欲归因不是同一种解释。\nAmerican inequality means that the gains of increasing productivity are not widely shared\nby everyone", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "An economist's prediction about a shorter average working week seemed to be correct for a time in the 20th century.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】某位经济学家关于更短周工时的预测在 20 世纪曾一度看似正确\n【定位】How will we all keep busy when we only have to work 15 hours a week? That was the\nquestion that worried the British economist John Maynard Keynes when he wrote his short\nessay \"Economic Possibilities for Our Grandchildren\" in 1930. Over the next century, he\npredicted, the economy would become so productive that people would barely need to work\nat all. For a while, it looked like Keynes was right. In 1930 the average working week was 47\nhours in the United States. But by 1970, the number of hours Americans worked on average\nhad fallen to slightly less than 39.\n当我们一周只需工作 15 小时时，我们要拿什么来打发时间？这是 1930 年英国经济学家凯\n恩斯在写下短文《我们孙辈的经济可能性》时心里担忧的问题。他预言在接下来的一个世纪\n里，经济会变得高产到人几乎不必工作。有一段时间看起来凯恩斯说对了——1930 年美国\n的平均周工时是 47 小时，但到 1970 年已下降到略低于 39 小时。\n【改写】An economist's prediction about a shorter average working week ↔\nhe predicted, the economy would become so productive that people would barely need to work\nat all\n概括上位 + 名词化\nseemed to be correct for a time in the 20th century ↔\nFor a while, it looked like Keynes was right 与 In 1930 the average working week was 47 hours\n与 by 1970, the number of hours Americans worked on average had fallen to slightly less than\n时间副词换 + 具体年份概括为世纪\n【辨邻】 C C 段同样肯定凯恩斯的预测，但对的是 Technology has made the economy\nmassively more productive 这条技术-生产率预测，而非陈述句要的 a shorter\naverage working week 周工时缩短预测。\nTechnology has made the economy massively more productive", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "In the U.S. labor market, the primary concern of people with less schooling and fewer skills is to secure any employment even if it is low-paid.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】受教育与技能较少者最在意能找到任何工作、哪怕低薪\n【定位】Americans now have a labor market in which millions of people—those with fewer skills and\nless education—are seeking whatever poorly paid work they can get. This is confirmed by a\nrecent poll that found that, for half of hourly workers, their top concern is not that they work\ntoo much but that they work too little.\n美国现在的劳动力市场上，数以百万计的人——技能较少、受教育较少的人——正在寻找任\n何能找到的低薪工作。最近一项民意调查也证实了这一点：在时薪工人中，有一半人最担心\n的不是自己工作太多，而是工作太少。\n【改写】people with less schooling and fewer skills ↔ those with fewer skills and less education\n同义词替换 + 语序互换\nthe primary concern ↔ their top concern\n同义词替换\nto secure any employment even if it is low-paid ↔\nseeking whatever poorly paid work they can get 与 their top concern is … that they work too\nlittle\n同义动词替换 + 让步逻辑同义\n【辨邻】 H H 段虽然也讲美国普通劳动者，但用的描述是 normal workers (those who were not\nin management roles) 与 ordinary workers，讨论的是时薪购买力下降，与陈述句\n要的 people with less schooling and fewer skills 与 to secure any employment\neven if it is low-paid 不在一个对象与目标上。\nbetween 1947 and 1973 the average hourly wage for normal workers", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Keynes was right in predicting that technology would make the economy much more productive.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】凯恩斯关于技术让经济大幅增产的预测被证实\n【定位】There would be no mystery in this if Keynes had been wrong about the power of technology\nto increase the economy's productivity, which he thought would lead to a standard of living\n\"between four and eight times as high as it is today.\" But Keynes got that right: Technology\nhas made the economy massively more productive.\n如果凯恩斯关于技术能提升经济生产率的判断是错的，那这件事就没什么神秘的了——他当\n年认为这种技术力量会让生活水平比今天高四到八倍。但凯恩斯这一点说对了：技术已经让\n经济的生产率大幅提升。\n【改写】Keynes was right in predicting ↔ But Keynes got that right\n同义短语\ntechnology would make the economy much more productive ↔\nTechnology has made the economy massively more productive\n时态切换 + 副词同义\n【辨邻】 A A 段虽然也涉及凯恩斯，但对的是 he predicted, the economy would become so\nproductive that people would barely need to work at all 这一工时预测的早期成立\n期；陈述句要的是技术-生产率本身被证实，对位的是 C 段的 Technology has made\nthe economy massively more productive。\nhe predicted, the economy would become so productive that people would barely need to work\nat all\nG G 段虽然也提凯恩斯，但讨论的是 Keynes's prediction of a shorter working week\nrests on the idea that the standard of living would continue rising for everyone\n与该预测对中位数工人不成立的反例，态度是部分否定；陈述句要的是技术-生产率预\n测被肯定，方向相反。\nBut Friedman says that this is not what has happened", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Many of the highest earners have a keen interest in and love for what they are doing.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】许多最高收入者对自己所做的事抱有浓厚兴趣与热爱\n【定位】Friedman believes that for many top earners, work is a labor of love. They are doing work\nthey care about and are interested in, and doing more of it is not necessarily a burden.\n弗里德曼认为，对很多顶层高收入者来说，工作就是出于爱的劳动；他们在做自己关心、感\n兴趣的事，多做一点也未必是负担。\n【改写】Many of the highest earners ↔ the highest earners in America 与 for many top earners\n同义词替换\na keen interest in and love for what they are doing ↔\nwork is a labor of love 与 They are doing work they care about and are interested in\n习语展开 + 同义改写\n【辨邻】 I I 段讨论的是 those with fewer skills and less education，他们的态度是 we can\nassume it is because they need the money——出于需要钱，而非陈述句要的 a\nkeen interest in and love for what they are doing 出于热爱。\nwe can assume it is because they need the money", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "According to Keynes, there would be a shorter working week with everyone's standard of living continuing to rise.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】凯恩斯认为周工时缩短的同时所有人生活水平继续上升\n【定位】Keynes's prediction of a shorter working week rests on the idea that the standard of living\nwould continue rising for everyone.\n凯恩斯关于周工时会缩短的预测，建立在所有人生活水平会持续上升这一前提之上。\n【改写】According to Keynes, there would be a shorter working week ↔\nKeynes's prediction of a shorter working week\n态度归属 + 名词化\neveryone's standard of living continuing to rise ↔\nthe standard of living would continue rising for everyone\n句式重构 + 主语位置互换\n【辨邻】 A A 段虽然也讲凯恩斯与更短周工时，但表达的是 For a while, it looked like Keynes\nwas right 与 he predicted, the economy would become so productive that\npeople would barely need to work at all，没有出现 everyone's standard of living\ncontinuing to rise 这一前提条件。\nhe predicted, the economy would become so productive that people would barely need to work\nat all\nC C 段虽然也提到凯恩斯所谓的生活水平 between four and eight times as high as it\nis today，但主线是 Technology has made the economy massively more\nproductive 这一技术-生产率预测的成立，没有把 a shorter working week 与\neveryone's standard of living continuing to rise 这两块信息绑在一起。\nTechnology has made the economy massively more productive", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What can we conclude from Lao Zi's quotation?", "options": ["A) We should see through other people's attempt to make a prisoner of us.", "B) We can never really please other people even if we try as hard as we can.", "C) We can never be truly free if taking to heart others' opinion of us.", "D) We should care about other people's view as much as they care about our own."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （推断题）\n【判型】题干What can we conclude from Lao Zi's quotation?含conclude，要求由引语推出结\n论，而非复述字面。\n【拆句】题干What can we conclude from Lao Zi's quotation?是常规 wh- 疑问句，主干为 What\ncan we conclude，from 介词短语修饰 conclude，结构平直，无陷阱。\n【定位】Lao Zi once said, \"Care about what other people think and you will always be their prisoner.\"\n老子曾说：在意别人怎么想，你就会永远做他们的囚徒。\n【选项】A) 把比喻当字面属偷换概念\nsee through other people's attempt to make a prisoner of us把 prisoner 当成真有\n人想囚禁我们，原文是比喻、不是有人主动加害。\nB) 张冠李戴\nWe can never really please other people even if we try as hard as we can.说的是\n讨好不可能成功，这是If it's true that you can't please all people all of the time的内\n容，不是P1老子那句引语的结论。\nC) ✓ 正确\nCare about what other people think ↔ taking to heart others' opinion of us\nyou will always be their prisoner ↔ can never be truly free\nWe can never be truly free if taking to heart others' opinion of us.同时覆盖了原文\nif-then 链条的两端，是定位句的完整同义改写。\nD) 无中生有\nas much as they care about our own提出一种对等关心的主张，但P1引语只说在意他\n人会失自由，根本未涉及对方是否在意我们。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What will happen if we base our self-worth on other people's approval?", "options": ["A) Our desire to be loved will be fulfilled.", "B) Our life will be unfruitful and exhausting.", "C) Our identity as social beings will be affected.", "D) Our sense of self will be sharpened and enhanced."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干What will happen if we base our self-worth on other people's approval?问的是一\n种行为的直接后果，答案应来自原文对应描述句的客观转述。\n【拆句】题干What will happen if we base our self-worth on other people's approval?是 if 引\n导条件状语从句的疑问句，主干 What will happen，base A on B 是固定搭配，意为以B为\n基础。\n【定位】People-pleasing, or seeking self-worth through others' approval, is unproductive and an\nexhausting way to go through life.\n讨好他人，或者说通过他人的认可来寻求自我价值，是一种无成效且令人疲惫的活法。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文将该行为定性为 unproductive、exhausting，选项Our desire to be loved will\nbe fulfilled.却说愿望被满足，与原文极性相反。\nB) ✓ 正确\nunproductive ↔ unfruitful\nan exhausting way to go through life ↔ exhausting\nOur life will be unfruitful and exhausting.同时覆盖了原文两个评价形容词\nunproductive 与 exhausting，正合预判。\nC) 无中生有\nFor social beings who desire love and belonging是描述人之常情，原文并未说\nsocial beings 这一身份会因 people-pleasing 而被影响。\nD) 与原文相反\nwhere we get into trouble is when our self-worth is dependent upon whether we\nwin someone's approval or not说明此举使自我陷入麻烦，Our sense of self will be\nsharpened and enhanced.却说自我被强化，正好相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What may account for our need to be liked or approved of?", "options": ["A) Our desperate longing for interactions with others.", "B) Our understanding of the workings of the world.", "C) Our knowledge about the pain of abandonment.", "D) Our early childhood fear of being deserted."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干What may account for our need to be liked or approved of?问一种心理需求的成\n因，原文若给出明确解释句，答案即其同义改写。\n【拆句】题干What may account for our need to be liked or approved of?主语是 What，谓语\nmay account for，宾语为 our need to be liked or approved of。be liked 与 be\napproved of 是两个并列被动不定式，approved of 中 of 不可省，因 approve of 是固定\n及物动词短语。\n【定位】This need to be liked can be traced back to when we were children and were completely\ndependent on others to take care of us… We need to understand that when we desperately\nwant someone to approve of us, it's being driven by that little kid part of us that is still\nterrified of abandonment.\n这种想被喜欢的需求可以追溯到我们还是孩子、完全依赖他人照料的时候……我们要明白，\n当我们极度渴望得到他人认可时，背后真正驱动的，是那个仍然害怕被遗弃的小小孩子。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nWe learn about who we are and what is expected of us based on interactions\nwith others里 interactions 是孩子学习自我的途径，不是渴望被喜欢的成因。\nB) 偷换概念\nthey are also trying to learn how the world works说孩子在学习世界运转方式，这是\n孩子在做的事，不是想被喜欢的成因。\nC) 偷换概念\nknowledge about the pain of abandonment说我们已经知道被遗弃的痛苦；原文实\n际说的是儿童害怕被遗弃 still terrified of abandonment，是恐惧而非已掌握的知识。\nD) ✓ 正确\nwhen we were children ↔ early childhood\nterrified of abandonment ↔ fear of being deserted\nOur early childhood fear of being deserted.同时锁住了童年这个时间端与害怕被遗弃\n这个情感端，与原文因果链根源端完全对应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What can we do when we become better able to provide ourselves with the desired approval?", "options": ["A) Enjoy a happier life.", "B) Exercise self-restraint.", "C) Receive more external validation.", "D) Strengthen our power of imagination."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干What can we do when we become better able to provide ourselves with the\ndesired approval?问的是某条件下发生的事/可获得的结果，属直接的客观陈述。\n【拆句】题干What can we do when we become better able to provide ourselves with the\ndesired approval?主句是 What can we do，when 引导时间状语从句。从句主干 we\nbecome…able to…，better 修饰形容词 able。provide sb. with sth. 是固定搭配，意为\n给…提供…。\n【定位】As you become more capable of providing yourself with the approval you seek, your need for\nexternal validation will start to vanish, leaving you stronger, more confident, and yes, happier\nin your life.\n当你越来越能给自己所寻求的认可时，你对外部认可的需要会开始消退，让你变得更强、更\n自信、并且——是的——在生活中更幸福。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nhappier in your life ↔ happier life\nEnjoy a happier life.精准对应三个并列结果中的最后一项 happier in your life，且最直\n白、无歧义。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文we restrain our authentic selves是把克制真实自我描述为浪费时间的行为，不是\n该条件下我们应做的事；Exercise self-restraint.反而把原文要批判的行为当成了结果。\nC) 与原文相反\nyour need for external validation will start to vanish明确说对外部认可的需求会消\n退，Receive more external validation.却说获得更多，方向相反。\nD) 偷换概念\nImagine how much time we lose…是作者修辞性祈使，引导读者去想象浪费的时间，\n并不是说我们会去强化想象力。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author advise us to do in the last paragraph?", "options": ["A) Embrace life for ourselves and for others.", "B) Base our worth on others' opinions.", "C) See our experiences as assets.", "D) Love ourselves as we are."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干What does the author advise us to do in the last paragraph?限定段落+问具体建\n议，按位置直接精读末段祈使句即可。\n【拆句】题干What does the author advise us to do in the last paragraph?是 wh- 疑问句，主干\nWhat does the author advise us to do，advise sb. to do sth. 是固定搭配，in the last\nparagraph 是地点状语限定范围。\n【定位】So embrace the cliché(老话) and love yourself as it's highly doubtful that you'll regret it.\n所以拥抱这句老话——爱自己吧，你几乎不可能后悔。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nembrace life not only for others but also for ourselves说不仅为他人也为自己拥抱生\n活，是过渡论述，不是末段 So 之后给出的最终建议；选项Embrace life for ourselves\nand for others.把它包装成建议，越权代替了真正的祈使句。\nB) 与原文相反\nIf we base our worth on the opinions of others, we cheat ourselves of the power\nto shape our experiences明确指出以他人评价为基础是自我欺骗，作者反对这一做\n法，Base our worth on others' opinions.反而把它当建议。\nC) 无中生有\n原文the power to shape our experiences说的是塑造自身体验的力量，不是把体验视\n为财富。See our experiences as assets.借 experiences 一词制造表层相似，实则原文\n从未把它定义为 assets。\nD) ✓ 正确\nlove yourself ↔ Love ourselves as we are\nLove ourselves as we are.忠实保留了原文祈使动作 love+反身代词的结构，是末段建\n议的直译版。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do some people think of aging adults?", "options": ["A) Their wisdom grows as time goes by.", "B) Their memory gradually deteriorates.", "C) They can benefit from late-in-life learning.", "D) They are likely to have mental health issues."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do some people think of aging adults? 用 what do ... think of 直接问看法，且明\n确限定主语 some people，属于对原文具体观点的事实复述。\n【拆句】Q51 主干为 What do some people think of aging adults，think of 表示「对……的看\n法」，think 与 of 分立但语义绑定，基础薄弱考生易把 think of 拆开理解为「想到」，注\n意它是「评价」之义。\n【定位】Some people have said aging is more a slide into forgetfulness than a journey\ntowards wisdom.\n某些人说，衰老与其说是一段通向智慧的旅程，不如说是一步步滑向健忘。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n题干问 some people 的看法，原文 some people 恰恰用 more A than B 否定了「通\n向智慧」这一端，Their wisdom grows as time goes by. 正好与之相反。\nB) ✓ 正确\na slide into forgetfulness ↔ memory gradually deteriorates\n预判「记性变差」与 Their memory gradually deteriorates. 的语义完全一致，且整句\n都被原文 slide into forgetfulness 覆盖，无添加成分。\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey can benefit from late-in-life learning. 是作者及研究方的观点（a growing\nbody of research suggests that late-in-life learning is possible），不属于题干所问\n的 some people。\nD) 无中生有\n首段并未提及 some people 认为衰老会导致 mental health issues（精神健康问\n题），原文只说「健忘」（forgetfulness），健忘不等于心理疾病。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What can we conclude from Geoffrey Raisman's finding?", "options": ["A) Brain damage seriously hinders one's learning.", "B) Brain power weakens slower than we imagine.", "C) Brains can refresh and improve with learning.", "D) Brains forge connections under new conditions."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （推断题）\n【判型】What can we conclude from Geoffrey Raisman's finding? 出现 conclude，要求从已述\n事实再推一步得出结论。\n【拆句】Q52 句中 what 是 conclude 的宾语，from Geoffrey Raisman's finding 是状语，结构平\n铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】In the late 1960s, British brain scientist Geoffrey Raisman spied growth in damaged brain\nregions of rats through an electron microscope; their brains were forging new connections.\nThis meant brains may change every time a person learns something new.\n上世纪 60 年代末，英国脑科学家 Geoffrey Raisman 用电子显微镜观察到老鼠受损脑区长\n出新生组织；它们的大脑正在缔结新连接。这意味着，每当一个人学到新东西，大脑都可能\n发生改变。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文只说 Raisman 在「受损脑区」观察到新连接，并未谈论 brain damage 对\nlearning 的影响，更没有 hinders 这种负向判断。\nB) 无中生有\n原文未做「比想象慢/快」的速度比较，brain power weakens slower 这种比较关系在\nRaisman 段并不存在。\nC) ✓ 正确\nbrains may change every time a person learns something new ↔\nBrains can refresh and improve with learning.\n预判核心是「学习—大脑改变/更新」，Brains can refresh and improve with\nlearning. 用 refresh、improve 概括 change + forging new connections 的双层含\n义，且 with learning 直接对应「每当学新东西」，整句无溢出。\nD) 答非所问\nBrains forge connections under new conditions. 几乎照搬 their brains were\nforging new connections，但题干问的是 conclude（结论意义），而该选项只重复了\n实验事实，没把意义说出来。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What is one thing that helps maintain the health of our brain even as it shrinks?", "options": ["A) Doing daily routines by conventional means.", "B) Avoiding worrying about our mental durability.", "C) Imitating old dogs' way of learning new tricks.", "D) Approaching everyday tasks in novel ways."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is one thing that helps maintain the health of our brain 用 one thing that ... 直\n接问一件具体做法，属事实复述。\n【拆句】Q53 主干 What is one thing，其后 that ... 是定语从句修饰 thing；even as it shrinks\n是让步状语，as 此处等于 although，it 指代 brain，须留意 it 的指代关系不要错认\n成 health。\n【定位】Even something as simple as taking a different route to the grocery store or going somewhere\nnew on vacation can keep the brain healthy.\n哪怕是像换条路去超市、或换个地方度假这样简单的事，也能让大脑保持健康。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文强调要「换新方式」做日常事，而 Doing daily routines by conventional means.\n强调用 conventional means（常规方式），方向完全相反。\nB) 无中生有\nAvoiding worrying about our mental durability. 提到「避免担忧脑力耐久度」，但原\n文 P5 谈的是「具体做法」，从未谈论「担忧」或「避免担忧」这个心理层面。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文末段 It\\'s not that old dogs can\\'t learn new tricks. It\\'s that maybe old dogs\ndon\\'t realize why they should. 中的 old dogs 是比喻人，不是真的「狗的学习方\n式」；Imitating old dogs\\' way of learning new tricks. 把比喻当字面，且并非 P5 的\n护脑手段。\nD) ✓ 正确\ntaking a different route to the grocery store or going somewhere new on vacation ↔\nApproaching everyday tasks in novel ways.\n预判的两个关键语义点「日常事」和「新方式」在 Approaching everyday tasks in\nnovel ways. 中一一对应，没有任何额外成分。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the author say can contribute to the improvement of brain function?", "options": ["A) Being curious and desiring knowledge.", "B) Being eager to pass brain tests at an old age.", "C) Rising to life's challenges and avoiding risks.", "D) Boosting immunity to serious mental diseases."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say can contribute to the improvement of brain function? 直\n接问 author 提出的「促进项」，是对原文表述的事实复述。\n【拆句】Q54 主干为 What does the author say can contribute to ...，what 既作 say 的宾语，又\n作 can contribute to 的逻辑主语（省略关系代词的嵌套从句结构），基础薄弱考生易在两\n个谓语间断错语义，建议拆为「作者说，[什么]能促进脑功能提升」。\n【定位】Openness—a characteristic defined by curiosity and a desire for knowledge—may also help\nfolks pass brain tests.\n「开放性」——一种由好奇心和求知欲所定义的特质——也可以帮助人们顺利通过脑力\n测试。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ncuriosity and a desire for knowledge ↔ Being curious and desiring knowledge.\n预判的两个核心要素「好奇心」和「求知欲」在 Being curious and desiring\nknowledge. 中由同根词分别承接，且选项整体只表达这两点，无多余成分，无遗漏。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文说 openness 能帮助 pass brain tests，是「特质 → 通过测试」的因果；Being\neager to pass brain tests at an old age. 把「通过测试」偷换成「渴望通过测试」，\n把结果偷换成动机，方向不同。\nC) 无中生有\n原文 A desire for new life challenges can further boost brainpower. 只说「迎接新\n挑战」能提升脑力，从未提到 avoiding risks（规避风险）；C 项硬加了「规避风险」\n这一成分。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 improved function and memory as well as a reduced risk of mental disease\n中，reduced risk of mental disease 是 take on new enterprises 带来的「结果之\n一」，而非「促进脑功能的手段」；Boosting immunity to serious mental diseases.\n把这一结果倒置成促进手段。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What is the finding of the 2012 study in the journal Psychology and Aging?", "options": ["A) Wishing to solve puzzles enhances one's reasoning power.", "B) Playing number games unexpectedly stimulates one's memory.", "C) Desiring new experiences can help to renovate the brain.", "D) Learning new tricks should not be confined to old dogs only."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is the finding of the 2012 study 直接问研究发现内容，属定位+复述型问法。\n【拆句】Q55 主干为 What is the finding，of the 2012 study 与 in the journal Psychology and\nAging 都是修饰 finding 的介词短语，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】While genes can encourage an interest in doing new things, a 2012 study in the journal\nPsychology and Aging found completing reasoning tasks like puzzles and number games can\nenhance that desire for novel experiences, which can, in turn, refresh the brain.\n尽管基因能在一定程度上鼓励一个人对新事物的兴趣，但 2012 年发表在《Psychology\nand Aging》期刊上的一项研究发现，完成像谜题、数字游戏这样的推理任务能够强化「想\n要新体验」的欲望，而这种欲望反过来又能刷新大脑。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文是「做推理任务（含 puzzles）→ 强化对新体验的欲望 → 刷新大脑」，Wishing\nto solve puzzles enhances one\\'s reasoning power. 反过来变成「想做谜题 → 提升\n推理力」，方向被颠倒，且原文从未谈 enhance reasoning power。\nB) 无中生有\n原文链条终点是 refresh the brain（刷新大脑），不是 stimulates one\\'s memory\n（刺激记忆）；且 unexpectedly（出乎意料地）也不在原文里。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthat desire for novel experiences, which can, in turn, refresh the brain ↔\nDesiring new experiences can help to renovate the brain.\n预判的落脚点「对新体验的欲望能刷新大脑」与 Desiring new experiences can help\nto renovate the brain. 在主语、动作、对象上一一对位，无添加成分。\nD) 张冠李戴\nLearning new tricks should not be confined to old dogs only. 改写自结尾的\nRichard Kennedy 引语，是 brain scientist Kennedy 的比喻金句，而非 2012 年那项\n研究的 finding。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
